QB.add(5,[{"id":5001,"t":"Starting Observations","s":"Despite gains in life expectancy and an NHS, which of the following is NOT a reason that medicine alone does not explain health?","o":["The NHS has removed health inequalities within the UK","There are enduring inequalities despite an NHS","There is a distinction between life expectancy and healthy life expectancy","Patients use the health service in ways that contradict medical expectations","Many social, cultural, economic, psychological, biological and environmental factors shape health"],"e":"The slide lists four observations that persist <b>despite</b> modern medicine and <b>despite an NHS</b>: <b>enduring inequalities</b>, a gap between <b>LE and healthy LE</b>, patients using services in ways that <b>contradict medical expectations and assumptions</b>, and an array of <b>social, cultural, historical, economic, psychological, biological and environmental</b> factors shaping health. The lecturer stressed that the NHS is free at the point of access, yet inequalities are still seen across UK populations, so the claim that it has removed them is the opposite of the lecture's point.","w":["This is one of the starting observations: inequalities endure even with an NHS that is free at the point of access.","This is listed: some populations have both a lower life expectancy and a younger age at which healthy life ends.","This is listed: people vary in how they respond to advice, appointments and different doctors.","This is listed: the seven factor types (SCHEPBE) shape health status."],"k":"Despite medicine and the NHS: inequalities, LE vs healthy LE, unexpected service use, many shaping factors.","rn":[["","What 4 observations show medicine is not the whole story?","What 4 observations show medicine is not the whole story?","Enduring inequalities despite the NHS; Gap between life expectancy and healthy life expectancy; Patients use services in ways that contradict medical expectations; Many factors shape health status"],["","Why is the NHS (National Health Service) not enough to remove health inequalities?","Why is the NHS (National Health Service) not enough to remove health inequalities?","Inequalities between UK populations persist; Even though care is free at the point of access"]]},{"id":5002,"t":"Starting Observations","s":"Two populations have similar NHS access. Population A has a lower life expectancy and also reaches the age at which illness takes over earlier. Which concept does this illustrate?","o":["The distinction between life expectancy and healthy life expectancy","The distinction between disease and illness in lay conceptions","The doctrine of specific aetiology in the biomedical model","The distinction between functional fitness and absence of illness","The shift in causes of death across the twentieth century"],"e":"The lecture highlighted the <b>distinction between LE and healthy LE</b>: why do some populations have a <b>lower life expectancy</b> and also a <b>lower age at which their healthy life ends</b>, before illness or infirmity takes over? The scenario describes both a shorter life and fewer healthy years in population A, despite equal access to care, which is exactly this observation.","w":["Disease vs illness is about objective vs subjective, not population age patterns.","Specific aetiology is about one cause per disease.","Functional fitness is a lay conception of health.","That is about causes of death, not healthy life years."],"k":"LE vs healthy LE: years lived vs years lived in health.","rn":[["","Healthy life expectancy","Healthy life expectancy","Years lived in health before illness or infirmity takes over"]]},{"id":5003,"t":"Health, Illness, Disease and Sickness","s":"Which statement best reflects the WHO (2020) definition of health?","o":["Complete physical, mental and social well-being, not merely absence of disease","Complete physical and mental well-being, not merely absence of disease","The absence of disease and infirmity, with good mental well-being","Complete physical, mental and social well-being, including absence of disease","The ability to carry on with normal activities despite disease"],"e":"WHO (2020): health is <b>a state of complete physical, mental and social well-being and not merely the absence of disease or infirmity</b>. The lecturer drew attention to two features: it goes beyond the absence of disease, and it explicitly includes <b>social</b> factors, which fits the biopsychosocial approach of the lecture.","w":["WHO includes social well-being.","WHO says “not merely” the absence of disease.","The point is “not merely” the absence of disease.","That is the lay conception of functional fitness."],"k":"WHO 2020: complete physical, mental and social well-being; not merely absence of disease.","rn":[["","What is the WHO (2020) definition of health?","What is the WHO (2020) definition of health?","A state of complete physical, mental and social well-being; Not merely the absence of disease or infirmity"]]},{"id":5004,"t":"Health, Illness, Disease and Sickness","s":"Which pairing of lay conception of health and its description is correct?","o":["Absence of illness – no subjective symptoms","Absence of disease – no subjective symptoms","Functional fitness – no objective symptoms","Absence of illness – no objective symptoms","Functional fitness – complete social well-being"],"e":"Lay conceptions of health: <b>absence of disease</b> (no <b>objective</b> symptoms), <b>absence of illness</b> (no <b>subjective</b> symptoms), and <b>functional fitness</b> (<b>the ability to go on</b>, with a good quality of life). Memory aid: Disease = Detectable; Illness = I feel it; Functional fitness = Fit to carry on.","w":["Disease relates to objective symptoms.","Functional fitness is the ability to go on.","Illness relates to subjective symptoms.","That is part of the WHO definition, not functional fitness."],"k":"Disease = objective; illness = subjective; functional fitness = ability to go on.","rn":[["","Absence of illness: objective or subjective?","Absence of illness: objective or subjective?","Subjective symptoms"],["","Absence of disease: objective or subjective?","Absence of disease: objective or subjective?","Objective symptoms"]]},{"id":5005,"t":"Health, Illness, Disease and Sickness","s":"A man with an abnormal blood test feels completely well. A woman with no detectable abnormality feels unwell every day. Which statement is correct?","o":["The man has disease without illness; the woman has illness without disease","The man has illness without disease; the woman has disease without illness","Both have disease, because each has a health complaint","Both have illness, because each consulted a doctor","The man has sickness; the woman has disease"],"e":"<b>Disease</b> is something that can be <b>objectively classified</b> (objective symptoms or findings). <b>Illness</b> refers to <b>subjective</b> symptoms: how the person feels. The man has an objective finding without feeling unwell; the woman feels unwell without an objective finding. This shows why the terms are not interchangeable even though they are sometimes used that way.","w":["Reversed: disease is objective, illness subjective.","The woman has no objective finding.","The man feels well, so no illness.","Disease is the objective finding."],"k":"Disease (objective) can exist without illness (subjective), and vice versa.","rn":[["","Disease vs illness","Disease vs illness","Disease: objective, can be classified; Illness: subjective, what the person feels"]]},{"id":5006,"t":"Health, Illness, Disease and Sickness","s":"A student says “I felt sick when I saw my exam results.” What does this show about the term “sickness”?","o":["Sickness need not be medical; it can mean a feeling of discomfort","Sickness always refers to an objectively classifiable disease","Sickness is the WHO term for loss of social well-being","Sickness is the lay term for functional unfitness","Sickness refers only to subjective symptoms of infection"],"e":"The lecturer gave the example that “sickness” might refer to something that is <b>not always a medical condition</b>; it can mean a <b>feeling or sense of discomfort</b>, as in the slang “I feel sick about that”. This illustrates that health, illness, disease and sickness are sometimes used interchangeably and lack clear definitions.","w":["The example shows it is not always medical.","Not the WHO definition.","Functional fitness is separate.","Not limited to infection."],"k":"Sickness can be a feeling, not a medical condition.","rn":[["","Why is \"I feel sick about that\" not a disease?","Why is \"I feel sick about that\" not a disease?","Sickness can mean a feeling of discomfort; No disease symptoms need exist"],["","What can \"sickness\" mean?","What can \"sickness\" mean?","A medical condition; Or simply a feeling of discomfort"]]},{"id":5007,"t":"Health, Illness, Disease and Sickness","s":"Why is what counts as “normal” health difficult to define?","o":["Lay views vary with class, gender, ethnicity, disability and disease","The WHO definition is too narrow to cover the experience of most people in the UK","Doctors and patients use different objective tests to define normal","Health is mainly biological, so normal values vary between labs","Lay people define health only as the absence of disease"],"e":"The slide states that lay conceptions <b>can mean different things to different people – what is ‘normal’?</b> They are <b>influenced by class, gender, ethnicity, disability and disease profile</b>. The summary adds that understandings of health, illness, sickness and disease are <b>contested</b>, partly because of what each individual brings.","w":["The issue raised was variation in lay conceptions, not the WHO wording.","Not the reason given.","Not the reason given.","Lay conceptions include illness and functional fitness too."],"k":"“Normal” varies with class, gender, ethnicity, disability, disease profile.","rn":[["","What influences lay conceptions of health?","What influences lay conceptions of health?","Class; Gender; Ethnicity; Disability; Disease profile"],["","Why is it hard to say what is \"normal\" health?","Why is it hard to say what is \"normal\" health?","Means different things to different people; Class, gender, ethnicity, disability, disease profile"]]},{"id":5008,"t":"The Biomedical Model","f":"ph07","s":"In the biomedical model, which sequence is correct?","o":["Pathogenic stimulus → biochemical mechanisms → disease state → recovery, chronic condition or death","Biochemical mechanisms → pathogenic stimulus → disease state → recovery, chronic condition or death","Pathogenic stimulus → disease state → biochemical mechanisms → recovery, chronic condition or death","Pathogenic stimulus → biochemical mechanisms → treatment → recovery only","Social factors → biochemical mechanisms → disease state → recovery, chronic condition or death"],"e":"The biomedical model: <b>pathogenic stimulus → physiological/biochemical mechanisms → disease state →</b> one of three outcomes: <b>recovery</b>, <b>chronic condition</b> or <b>death</b>. <b>Treatment</b> acts at the disease state, and <b>prevention/screening</b> (shown with question marks) act earlier, at the stimulus. It is “a problem, a solution, and algorithms”.","w":["The stimulus comes first.","Mechanisms come before the disease state.","There are three outcomes, not recovery only.","The biomedical model does not start with social factors."],"k":"Biomedical flow: stimulus → mechanisms → disease → 3 outcomes.","rn":[["","What are the steps of the biomedical model?","What are the steps of the biomedical model?","Pathogenic stimulus; Physiological / biochemical mechanisms; Disease state (treatment acts here); Outcome"]]},{"id":5009,"t":"The Biomedical Model","f":"ph07","s":"In the biomedical model, at which point would prevention and screening act?","o":["At the pathogenic stimulus, before the disease state","At the disease state, alongside treatment","At the chronic condition stage, to manage it and prevent death","After recovery, to prevent relapse","At the biochemical mechanisms stage only"],"e":"On the slide, “<b>? prevention ? / Screening ?</b>” act at the <b>pathogenic stimulus</b>, before the disease state, while <b>treatment</b> acts at the disease state. The lecturer described this as perhaps doing something to prevent the stimulus or screening to get ahead of the condition.","w":["Treatment acts at the disease state.","They are shown before the disease state.","Not shown.","They are at the stimulus stage."],"k":"Prevention/screening act at the stimulus; treatment at the disease state.","rn":[["","Where do prevention and screening act in the biomedical model?","Where do prevention and screening act in the biomedical model?","At the pathogenic stimulus"],["","What are the steps of the biomedical model?","What are the steps of the biomedical model?","Pathogenic stimulus; Physiological / biochemical mechanisms; Disease state (treatment acts here); Outcome"]]},{"id":5010,"t":"The Biomedical Model","s":"The biomedical model treats the body as a machine that can be repaired. Name this assumption.","e":"The mechanical metaphor (“car mechanic” approach) is one of Nettleton’s five assumptions.","k":"Mechanical metaphor.","a":["mechanical metaphor","the mechanical metaphor","machine metaphor"],"rn":[["","What is \"mechanical metaphor\" in the biomedical model?","What is \"mechanical metaphor\" in the biomedical model?","Body treated as a machine that needs fixing"]]},{"id":5011,"t":"Neuroplasticity","s":"Which of the following is <b>not</b> one of Nettleton's (2021) five assumptions of the biomedical model?","o":["Setting the patient in their wider social context","Mind/body dualism (treating mind and body separately)","The mechanical metaphor","Prioritising technological responses","The doctrine of specific aetiology"],"e":"Nettleton's five assumptions: <b>mind/body dualism</b>; the body can be repaired (<b>mechanical metaphor</b>); prioritises <b>technological responses</b>; focuses on the biological (<b>reductionist</b>); and the <b>doctrine of specific aetiology</b> (from germ theory). Setting the patient in a wider social context is a <b>strength of the biopsychosocial model</b>, which is what the biomedical model misses.","w":["One of the five assumptions.","One of the five assumptions.","One of the five assumptions.","One of the five assumptions."],"k":"Nettleton: dualism, machine, technology, reductionism, specific aetiology.","rn":[["","What are the 4 summary points of the lecture?","What are the 4 summary points of the lecture?","Health, illness and disease are contested; Know the patient's wider context; Biopsychosocial model enables reflexive, patient-centred practice; Social and psychological influences offer opportunities for medicine"],["","What are the layers of the \"setting biology in context\" model, centre outwards?","What are the layers of the \"setting biology in context\" model, centre outwards?","Age, sex and constitutional factors; Individual lifestyle factors; Social and community networks; Living and working conditions; General socio-economic, cultural and environmental conditions"],["","What are the 4 strengths of the biopsychosocial model?","What are the 4 strengths of the biopsychosocial model?","Holistic: patient in wider context; Empathetic practice and communication toolkit; Patient-centred care and reflexivity; Tailored advice"]]},{"id":5012,"t":"The Biomedical Model","s":"Name the doctrine, derived from germ theory, that every disease has one specific cause or biological process.","e":"The doctrine of specific aetiology (from germ theory): every disease has a specific cause, e.g. a specific virus or bacterium. It is one of Nettleton’s five biomedical assumptions.","k":"Specific aetiology.","a":["specific aetiology","doctrine of specific aetiology","specific etiology"],"rn":[["","Doctrine of specific aetiology","Doctrine of specific aetiology","Every disease has one specific cause; From germ theory"],["","What is \"doctrine of specific aetiology\"?","What is \"doctrine of specific aetiology\"?","Every disease has a specific cause; Or a specific biological process"]]},{"id":5013,"t":"The Biomedical Model","s":"A doctor assumes a patient’s family stress cannot affect recovery from surgery. Name the biomedical assumption that treats mind and body separately.","e":"Mind/body dualism treats the mind and body separately, assuming psychology and life events do not affect the body.","k":"Mind/body dualism.","a":["dualism","mind body dualism","mind/body dualism","mind-body dualism","cartesian dualism"],"rn":[["","Mind/body dualism","Mind/body dualism","Treating mind and body as separate"],["","What does the bio + psych overlap show about mind and body?","What does the bio + psych overlap show about mind and body?","Mind-body monism, not dualism"]]},{"id":5014,"t":"The Biomedical Model","s":"A health system invests heavily in new scanners and surgical robots but little in understanding patients' circumstances. Which biomedical assumption is most evident?","o":["Prioritising technological responses","The mechanical metaphor","Mind/body dualism","The doctrine of specific aetiology","Reductionism"],"e":"The biomedical model <b>prioritises technological responses</b>: “we have the technology to fix whatever the problem might be”. The scenario reflects investing in technology rather than in the wider context the biopsychosocial model emphasises.","w":["Close, but the scenario emphasises investment in technology.","Dualism is mind vs body.","Specific aetiology is one cause per disease.","Reductionism is focusing on biology; the scenario highlights technology."],"k":"Technological responses prioritised in the biomedical model.","rn":[["","What does \"prioritises technological responses\" mean?","What does \"prioritises technological responses\" mean?","Assume technology can fix the problem"]]},{"id":5015,"t":"Neuroplasticity","s":"Which statement best summarises the value of the biomedical model?","o":["It has huge value, but it misses psychological and social influences","It has huge value and explains the changing pattern of disease","It has little value now that the biopsychosocial model exists","It has huge value, but only for mental health conditions","It has little value because it ignores biology"],"e":"The slide asks: “<b>Huge value, but what does it miss?</b>” The model has brought major gains (e.g. treatment of acute disease, antibiotics), but its reductionism misses the psychological and social factors that the biopsychosocial model adds.","w":["It could not explain the rise in circulatory disease.","The lecturer said it has huge value.","Its gains were in acute disease, e.g. antibiotics.","It is focused on biology."],"k":"Biomedical: huge value but incomplete.","rn":[["","What are the 4 summary points of the lecture?","What are the 4 summary points of the lecture?","Health, illness and disease are contested; Know the patient's wider context; Biopsychosocial model enables reflexive, patient-centred practice; Social and psychological influences offer opportunities for medicine"],["","What did George Engel's model add to the biomedical model?","What did George Engel's model add to the biomedical model?","Social factors; Psychological factors"]]},{"id":5016,"t":"The Biomedical Model","f":"ph09","s":"Between 1900, 1950 and 1997, how did the total number of deaths in England and Wales change?","o":["It did not change dramatically","It roughly halved between 1900 and 1997","It rose steadily from 1900 to 1997","It fell steadily from 1900 to 1997","It was lowest in 1900 and highest in 1997"],"e":"The totals were <b>1900: 587,830</b>, <b>1950: 510,526</b>, <b>1997: 558,052</b>. The lecturer pointed out the number of deaths <b>does not change dramatically</b>; what changes is the <b>causes</b> of death. (As the population grew, deaths were proportionally fewer in 1997.)","w":["587,830 vs 558,052 is not halved.","1950 was lowest.","1997 was higher than 1950.","1900 had the most deaths."],"k":"Death totals stable; causes changed.","rn":[["","How did the number of deaths change from 1900 to 1997?","How did the number of deaths change from 1900 to 1997?","Little change in number each year; Proportionally fewer as population grew"]]},{"id":5017,"t":"The Biomedical Model","f":"ph09","s":"How did the causes of death in England and Wales change between 1900 and 1997?","o":["Infectious fell; circulatory and cancer rose; injury about the same","Infectious fell; circulatory fell; cancer and injury rose","Infectious rose; circulatory and cancer fell; injury about the same","Infectious fell; circulatory and cancer rose; injury rose sharply","All causes fell in roughly equal proportions"],"e":"In 1900 there were high levels of <b>infectious disease</b>, falling by the 1950s and through to 1997 (hygiene, vaccines, antibiotics). Meanwhile <b>circulatory disorders and cancer increased</b>, while <b>injury and poisoning essentially stayed the same</b>.","w":["Circulatory disease rose.","Infectious disease fell.","Injury/poisoning stayed about the same.","The total did not change dramatically; causes changed."],"k":"1900→1997: infection ↓, circulatory and cancer ↑.","rn":[["","How did causes of death change from 1900 to 1997?","How did causes of death change from 1900 to 1997?","Infectious disease fell; Circulatory disease and cancer rose; Injury and poisoning stayed similar"]]},{"id":5018,"t":"The Biomedical Model","s":"Which factors reduced deaths from infectious disease over the twentieth century?","o":["Hygiene, vaccines and antibiotic use","Hygiene, vaccines and better diets","Vaccines, antibiotics and less stress","Hygiene, screening and surgery","Antibiotics, technology and the NHS"],"e":"A student suggested vaccines and hygiene awareness, and the lecturer agreed: <b>hygiene, vaccines and antibiotic use</b> reduced infectious disease.","w":["Diet was linked to circulatory disease.","Stress was linked to circulatory disease.","Not the factors given.","Not the factors given."],"k":"Infection ↓ due to hygiene, vaccines, antibiotics.","rn":[["","Why did infectious disease deaths fall?","Why did infectious disease deaths fall?","Hygiene; Vaccines; Antibiotics"]]},{"id":5019,"t":"The Biomedical Model","s":"Why does the rise in circulatory disease from the 1950s suggest the biomedical model is not enough?","o":["It is shaped by work, stress, environment, poverty and diet","It was caused by a single newly identified pathogen","It was caused by better diagnostic technology","It shows that the doctrine of specific aetiology is correct after all","It reflects the fall in infectious disease deaths only"],"e":"The slide states the changing disease profile is <b>not simply the result of biochemical factors</b>; it is influenced by <b>work, stress, environment, poverty</b> (and, in the recording, the types of diets people eat). So we need to consider a person's <b>behaviour and wider societal factors</b> – the case for Engel's biopsychosocial model.","w":["That would fit the biomedical model.","Not the explanation.","It challenges specific aetiology.","Not the reasoning given."],"k":"Changing disease profile needs behavioural and societal explanations.","rn":[["","What else explains the changing disease profile?","What else explains the changing disease profile?","Work, stress, environment, poverty, diet; Not just biochemical factors"],["","Why is the fall in deaths not just biochemical?","Why is the fall in deaths not just biochemical?","Technology explains only some; Work, stress, environment, poverty, diet matter"]]},{"id":5020,"t":"The Biopsychosocial Model","s":"Who proposed the biopsychosocial model, adding psychological and social factors to the biomedical approach?","e":"George Engel proposed the biopsychosocial model, incorporating psychological and social factors alongside biology.","k":"George Engel.","a":["engel","george engel","g engel"],"rn":[["","Who proposed the biopsychosocial model?","Who proposed the biopsychosocial model?","George Engel"],["","What did George Engel's model add to the biomedical model?","What did George Engel's model add to the biomedical model?","Social factors; Psychological factors"]]},{"id":5021,"t":"The Biopsychosocial Model","f":"ph12","s":"In the biopsychosocial model, substance abuse is placed in the overlap of which two domains, and why?","o":["Biological and social: predisposition needs access to the substance","Biological and psychological: predisposition needs a coping style","Psychological and social: it depends on life events","Biological only: it reflects genetic susceptibility","Social only: it depends entirely on access"],"e":"Substance abuse is in the <b>bio + social</b> overlap. There may be a <b>biological predisposition</b> (e.g. risk-taking or genetic susceptibility to alcohol), but if a person <b>never has access</b> to the substance there will be no abuse of it: the social environment also matters.","w":["It is placed in bio + social.","Life events are in psych + social.","Access (social) is also needed.","Biological predisposition also matters."],"k":"Substance abuse = bio + social (predisposition + access).","rn":[["","Why is substance abuse bio + social?","Why is substance abuse bio + social?","Needs a biological predisposition; And access to the substance (social environment)"]]},{"id":5022,"t":"The Biopsychosocial Model","f":"ph12","s":"Which items sit in the overlap of the biological and psychological domains?","o":["IQ and temperament","Substance abuse","Family relationships and life events","Socioeconomic status and education","Self-esteem and emotions"],"e":"<b>IQ and temperament</b> sit in the bio + psych overlap. The lecturer used this to show that psychology and biology are linked: <b>mind-body monism, not dualism</b>.","w":["Bio + social.","Social + psych.","Social circle.","Psych circle."],"k":"Bio + psych overlap: IQ, temperament.","rn":[["","IQ and temperament sit in which overlap?","IQ and temperament sit in which overlap?","Biological + psychological"]]},{"id":5023,"t":"The Biopsychosocial Model","f":"ph12","s":"Which of the following belongs in the social domain of the biopsychosocial model?","o":["Socioeconomic status","Stress reactivity","Coping and social skills","Neurochemistry","Genetic vulnerability"],"e":"The SOCIAL circle: <b>education, social support, peer relationships, family background, socioeconomic status</b> (“having a social foundation”).","w":["Bio circle.","Psych circle, despite the word “social”.","Bio circle.","Bio circle."],"k":"Social circle: education, support, peers, family background, SES.","rn":[["","Social circle examples?","Social circle examples?","Education; Social support; Peer relationships; Family background; Socioeconomic status"]]},{"id":5024,"t":"The Biopsychosocial Model","f":"ph12","s":"In the biopsychosocial model, “coping and social skills” belong to which domain?","o":["Psychological","Social","Biological","Bio + social overlap","The centre (well-being)"],"e":"The PSYCH circle contains <b>behaviour, personality, attitudes/beliefs, learning and memory, coping and social skills, self-esteem and emotions</b>. Despite the word “social”, coping and social skills are psychological.","w":["A common trap: the social circle is education, support, peers, family background, SES.","Gender, disability, physical health, neurochemistry, stress reactivity, genetics.","Substance abuse.","Well-being is in the centre."],"k":"Coping and social skills = psychological.","rn":[["","Which examples sit in the psychological circle?","Which examples sit in the psychological circle?","Behaviour; personality; Attitudes/beliefs; learning and memory; Coping and social skills; Self-esteem and emotions"],["","Psychological circle examples?","Psychological circle examples?","Behaviour; Personality; Attitudes / beliefs; Coping and social skills; Self-esteem and emotions"]]},{"id":5025,"t":"The Biopsychosocial Model","f":"ph13","s":"In the layered model setting biology in context, which layer lies closest to age, sex and constitutional factors?","o":["Individual lifestyle factors","Social and community networks","Living and working conditions","General socio-economic, cultural and environmental conditions","Health care services"],"e":"From the centre outwards: <b>age, sex and constitutional factors</b> → <b>individual lifestyle factors</b> → <b>social and community networks</b> → <b>living and working conditions</b> → <b>general socio-economic, cultural and environmental conditions</b>.","w":["Third layer.","Fourth layer.","Outermost layer.","Part of living and working conditions."],"k":"Rainbow: constitution → lifestyle → networks → living/working → socio-economic.","rn":[["","What are the layers of the \"setting biology in context\" model, centre outwards?","What are the layers of the \"setting biology in context\" model, centre outwards?","Age, sex and constitutional factors; Individual lifestyle factors; Social and community networks; Living and working conditions; General socio-economic, cultural and environmental conditions"]]},{"id":5026,"t":"The Biopsychosocial Model","f":"ph13","s":"Water and sanitation belong to which layer of the social model of health, and how do they affect health?","o":["Living and working conditions; fewer waterborne pathogens","Living and working conditions; drinking more water improves health","General socio-economic conditions; water prices push families into poverty","Individual lifestyle factors; hygiene habits prevent disease","Social and community networks; communities share water supplies"],"e":"Living and working conditions include agriculture and food production, education, work environment, unemployment, <b>water and sanitation</b>, health care services and housing. With better water supply and sanitation you will not have as many diseases caused by <b>pathogens in the water supply</b>.","w":["The link was pathogens in the water supply.","Water and sanitation sit in living and working conditions.","Water and sanitation are in living and working conditions.","Not the layer."],"k":"Water/sanitation (living and working conditions) reduce waterborne disease.","rn":[["","What are examples of living and working conditions?","What are examples of living and working conditions?","Agriculture and food; Education; Work environment, unemployment; Water and sanitation; Health care services; Housing"],["","Why does better water and sanitation reduce disease?","Why does better water and sanitation reduce disease?","Fewer diseases from pathogens in water"]]},{"id":5027,"t":"The Biopsychosocial Model","s":"Which item is NOT part of “living and working conditions” in the layered model of health?","o":["Social and community networks","Education","Water and sanitation","Unemployment","Health care services (e.g. GP access)"],"e":"Living and working conditions: agriculture and food production, <b>education</b>, work environment, <b>unemployment</b>, water and sanitation, <b>health care services</b> and <b>housing</b>. <b>Social and community networks</b> are a separate, inner layer.","w":["Listed in the layer.","Listed in the layer.","Listed in the layer.","Listed in the layer."],"k":"Networks are their own layer.","rn":[["","What is the third layer of the \"biology in context\" model?","What is the third layer of the \"biology in context\" model?","Social and community networks"],["","What are the layers of the \"setting biology in context\" model, centre outwards?","What are the layers of the \"setting biology in context\" model, centre outwards?","Age, sex and constitutional factors; Individual lifestyle factors; Social and community networks; Living and working conditions; General socio-economic, cultural and environmental conditions"]]},{"id":5028,"t":"Social Factors in Disease","s":"What do drapetomania and female hysteria illustrate?","o":["Diagnosis is socially and historically located","Diagnosis is shaped by the doctrine of specific aetiology","Diagnosis reflects the nocebo effect in patients","Diagnosis is mainly determined by biology","Diagnosis is validating rather than stigmatising"],"e":"The slide states <b>diagnosis and ways of seeing disease are socially and historically located</b>, e.g. <b>hysteria (Freud)</b> and <b>drapetomania (Cartwright)</b>. Hysteria was a “female condition” for women not coping (today perhaps recognised as postnatal depression); drapetomania was a “condition” attributed to slaves who wanted to escape, in the US before the Civil War.","w":["They show social construction of diagnosis.","Nocebo is about anticipated harm.","The point is the opposite.","These labels were harmful."],"k":"Hysteria and drapetomania: diagnosis is socially/historically located.","rn":[["","What does hysteria and drapetomania show about diagnosis?","What does hysteria and drapetomania show about diagnosis?","It is socially and historically located"]]},{"id":5029,"t":"Social Factors in Disease","s":"Name the historical US “diagnosis” given to enslaved people who wanted to escape.","e":"Drapetomania (Cartwright) shows diagnosis is socially and historically located; it was never a real condition.","k":"Drapetomania.","a":["drapetomania"],"rn":[["","What does hysteria and drapetomania show about diagnosis?","What does hysteria and drapetomania show about diagnosis?","It is socially and historically located"],["","What 2 historical diagnoses show diagnosis is socially located?","What 2 historical diagnoses show diagnosis is socially located?","Hysteria (Freud); Drapetomania (Cartwright)"]]},{"id":5030,"t":"Social Factors in Disease","s":"Women are less likely than men to be diagnosed with CHD, even though men consult less. Which explanation fits?","o":["Assumed lower risk in women, or atypical symptom descriptions","Women consult less often than men, so are seen less","Men are diagnosed more because they consult more often","CHD in women is mainly genetic and harder to detect","Women are referred straight to cardiologists, bypassing the GP entirely"],"e":"Men are <b>less likely to seek help</b>, yet women are still diagnosed less often. Possible social reasons given: an <b>assumption that women are less at risk</b>, or women <b>describing symptoms in a way that does not match the expected symptoms</b> of CHD. This shows social factors shaping diagnosis.","w":["Men are less likely to consult.","Men consult less, yet are diagnosed more.","Not the explanation.","Not stated."],"k":"CHD under-diagnosed in women: assumptions and symptom description.","rn":[["","Why might women be under-diagnosed with CHD?","Why might women be under-diagnosed with CHD?","Assumption women are less at risk; Symptoms described differently from the expected pattern"]]},{"id":5031,"t":"Social Factors in Disease","s":"Treatments for mental health vary by which factors?","o":["Socioeconomic status, gender and ethnicity","Socioeconomic status, age and disability","Gender, ethnicity and disease profile","Age, gender and geography","Socioeconomic status, education and religion"],"e":"The slide states <b>treatments for mental health vary by socioeconomic status, gender and ethnicity</b>. The recording gave the example of police reacting badly to people with mental health conditions or learning disabilities.","w":["Age and disability were not listed.","Disease profile was not listed.","Not listed.","Not listed."],"k":"Mental health treatment varies by SES, gender, ethnicity.","rn":[["","What affects mental health treatment?","What affects mental health treatment?","Socioeconomic status; Gender; Ethnicity"]]},{"id":5032,"t":"Social Factors in Disease","s":"During the Ebola outbreak, why did cultural meaning worsen transmission?","o":["Bodies were withheld from burial rites, so families hid deaths","Families refused vaccination because of conspiracy theories","Families preferred placebo treatments to hospital care","Red Cross workers diagnosed the illness as hysteria","Hospitals lacked the technology to isolate patients"],"e":"Bodies were not returned to families because of transmission risk, but preparing the dead for burial was very important. Families started <b>withholding bodies and not reporting deaths</b>, out of <b>distrust of Red Cross workers</b>, which made Ebola <b>spread further</b>. This shows how the social and cultural meaning of a disease affects experience and treatment.","w":["That relates to Covid-19.","Not described.","Not described.","Not the explanation."],"k":"Ebola: burial practices + distrust → hidden deaths → spread.","rn":[["","How did cultural meaning worsen Ebola?","How did cultural meaning worsen Ebola?","Bodies not returned for burial preparation; Families hid bodies and did not report deaths; Distrust of Red Cross workers spread disease"]]},{"id":5033,"t":"Social Factors in Disease","s":"What happened to Covid-19 death rates in populations with high vaccine uptake compared with vaccine-suspicious groups in the US?","o":["Higher uptake, fewer deaths; vaccine-suspicious US groups died more","Higher uptake made no difference to death rates in the US","Higher uptake meant higher death rates in vaccine-suspicious groups","Higher uptake mattered only in Ebola, not Covid-19","Higher uptake reduced deaths only where trust in doctors was already high"],"e":"Populations with <b>higher vaccine uptake did not die at the same rates</b>; in the <b>United States</b> large groups became very <b>vaccine-suspicious</b> and death rates were much higher. Trust and social meaning affect outcomes (the slide image showed “Covid 19 is a hoax, 5G is the killer” graffiti).","w":["It made a difference.","Reversed.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Covid-19: trust/uptake shaped death rates.","rn":[["","What did vaccine suspicion do in the US during Covid-19?","What did vaccine suspicion do in the US during Covid-19?","Much higher death rates in vaccine-suspicious groups"],["","How did COVID-19 vaccine uptake affect deaths?","How did COVID-19 vaccine uptake affect deaths?","Higher uptake: fewer COVID-19 deaths"]]},{"id":5034,"t":"Social Factors in Disease","s":"How does trust in doctor–patient relations affect treatment?","o":["It enhances therapy and adherence","It enhances diagnosis but has no effect on adherence","It reduces the placebo effect and so improves outcomes","It matters mainly in surgical specialties","It increases over-diagnosis of psychosomatic conditions in anxious patients"],"e":"The slide states <b>trust in doctor–patient relations enhances therapy/adherence</b>. The lecturer added that if trust is low, or other information is coming in (e.g. <b>conspiracy theories</b>), adherence and trust change.","w":["Trust enhances adherence.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Trust → better adherence.","rn":[["","What does trust in the doctor-patient relationship improve?","What does trust in the doctor-patient relationship improve?","Therapy and adherence"]]},{"id":5035,"t":"Social Factors in Disease","s":"Some adults now seek an ADHD or autism diagnosis to have their experience recognised. Is this label acting as stigmatising or validating?","e":"Diagnostic labels can be stigmatising or validating; with neurodiversity many seek a diagnosis to validate their experience.","k":"Validating.","a":["validating","validation","validate"],"rn":[["","Can diagnostic labels be validating?","Can diagnostic labels be validating?","Yes, or stigmatising; e.g. epilepsy, ADHD"],["","Give an example of a label becoming validating","Give an example of a label becoming validating","Autism / ADHD: once kept secret, now sought to validate"],["","How can a diagnosis be stigmatising and later validating?","How can a diagnosis be stigmatising and later validating?","Once kept secret or not talked about; Now sought to validate the condition"]]},{"id":5036,"t":"Pain, Placebo and Nocebo","f":"ph16","s":"In the biopsychosocial model of pain, which list contains only <b>psychological</b> factors?","o":["Mood/affect, catastrophizing, stress, coping","Disease severity, nociception, inflammation, brain function","Cultural factors, social environment, economic factors, social support","Nociception, stress, social support, coping","Brain function, mood, culture, inflammation"],"e":"Pain model: <b>biological</b> – disease severity, nociception, inflammation, brain function; <b>psychological</b> – mood/affect, catastrophizing, stress, coping; <b>social</b> – cultural factors, social environment, economic factors, social support.","w":["Biological.","Social.","Mixed.","Mixed."],"k":"Pain: bio / psych / social factor lists.","rn":[["","What are the psychological factors in pain?","What are the psychological factors in pain?","Mood / affect; Catastrophizing; Stress; Coping"]]},{"id":5037,"t":"Pain, Placebo and Nocebo","s":"In a study in rural Nepal, many people had back pain but did not attend available medical facilities. Why?","o":["Back pain was seen as part of old age, not a reason to see a doctor","Back pain was seen as a medical condition needing a specialist","There were no doctors or facilities available to attend","Back pain was seen as purely psychological and untreatable","Patients feared the side-effects of treatment"],"e":"In the rural Nepal study, when medical facilities were in place people did not attend, because back pain was seen as <b>“just a symptom of old age”</b>, not a medical condition. How people perceive pain differs with biological, social and psychological factors: “<b>It's not just about biology.</b>”","w":["The opposite.","Facilities were in place.","Not described.","Not described."],"k":"Cultural meaning of pain affects help-seeking.","rn":[["","Why did people in rural Nepal not attend for back pain?","Why did people in rural Nepal not attend for back pain?","Seen as \"just a symptom of old age\""],["","Is back pain always seen as a medical problem?","Is back pain always seen as a medical problem?","No, seen as part of ageing in rural Nepal; So facilities went unused"]]},{"id":5038,"t":"Pain, Placebo and Nocebo","s":"How does a calm, supportive environment in childbirth illustrate the biopsychosocial model of pain?","o":["A calm, supportive environment reduces the sensation of pain","A calm, supportive environment increases tolerance of medication","Pain in childbirth is mainly a placebo effect","Pain in childbirth is purely biological and fixed","Only medication can reduce pain in childbirth"],"e":"An emphasis on a <b>calm, supportive environment</b> reduces the sensations of pain in childbirth, showing that social and psychological factors influence the experience of pain.","w":["Not stated.","Not the claim.","It is influenced by environment.","Environment matters."],"k":"Environment changes pain experience.","rn":[["","What reduces pain in childbirth?","What reduces pain in childbirth?","A calm, supportive environment"],["","What does childbirth show about social factors and pain?","What does childbirth show about social factors and pain?","A calm, supportive setting lowers pain sensations"]]},{"id":5039,"t":"Pain, Placebo and Nocebo","s":"Which statement about the size of the placebo effect is correct?","o":["Beecher (1955) found 32%; later research found 50–60%","Beecher (1955) found 50–60%; later research found 32%","Beecher (1955) found 32%; later research found under 5%","Beecher (1955) found 5%; later research found 32%","Kirsch (1955) found 32%; later research found 50–60%"],"e":"Early studies: <b>32%</b> of patients (<b>Beecher, 1955</b>). Research since: <b>50–60%</b>. The placebo effect is a <b>measurable improvement in health not attributable to the treatment</b>.","w":["Reversed.","Later research found 50–60%.","Beecher found 32%.","It was Beecher; Kirsch studied Prozac."],"k":"Placebo: 32% (Beecher 1955) → 50–60%.","rn":[["","How common is the placebo effect?","How common is the placebo effect?","32% (Beecher, 1955); Later research: 50-60%"]]},{"id":5040,"t":"Pain, Placebo and Nocebo","s":"According to Rose et al. (2012), when is the placebo effect greater?","o":["When people choose their “treatment” and feel some control","When the treatment is given without the patient’s knowledge","When the doctor is highly trusted and chooses for them","When the treatment is expensive and hard to obtain","When patients are told the treatment is inert"],"e":"The effect is <b>greater if people choose “treatment”</b> (<b>Rose et al., 2012</b>). The lecturer explained that when people feel they have some <b>control</b>, they <b>improve more rapidly</b>.","w":["Choice increases it.","Choice by the patient was the factor.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Choice/control strengthens placebo.","rn":[["","When is the placebo effect greater?","When is the placebo effect greater?","When people choose the treatment (Rose et al., 2012)"],["","Why does choosing a treatment boost the placebo effect?","Why does choosing a treatment boost the placebo effect?","Feeling in control of treatment; People improve more rapidly"]]},{"id":5041,"t":"Pain, Placebo and Nocebo","s":"What did Kirsch conclude about Prozac?","o":["Prozac for mild depression is largely a placebo effect (Kirsch)","Prozac for severe depression is largely a placebo effect (Kirsch)","Prozac for mild depression is largely a nocebo effect (Kirsch)","Prozac for mild depression is largely a placebo effect (Beecher)","Prozac for mild depression is entirely biological (Kirsch)"],"e":"The slide states <b>Prozac for mild depression is largely a placebo effect</b> (<b>Kirsch</b>).","w":["Mild depression.","Placebo, not nocebo.","Kirsch, not Beecher.","Largely placebo."],"k":"Kirsch: Prozac in mild depression largely placebo.","rn":[["","What did Kirsch find about Prozac?","What did Kirsch find about Prozac?","For mild depression it is largely a placebo effect"]]},{"id":5042,"t":"Pain, Placebo and Nocebo","s":"A patient develops headaches after reading that a safe, inert tablet causes headaches. Name this effect.","e":"The nocebo effect: an inert substance produces symptoms congruent with anticipated harm.","k":"Nocebo.","a":["nocebo","nocebo effect"],"rn":[["","Nocebo effect","Nocebo effect","Inert substance produces symptoms congruent with anticipated harm"]]},{"id":5043,"t":"Pain, Placebo and Nocebo","s":"Why must drug trials control for the placebo effect?","o":["Patients in controlled trials still feel better regardless of the drug","Patients in controlled trials never experience placebo effects","Placebo effects are always harmful to trial participants","Placebo effects occur only in uncontrolled observational studies","Placebo effects occur only when patients choose their drug"],"e":"Some studies report approximate percentages, while others are <b>controlled drug trials where patients still report feeling better</b>, which is why trials must <b>control for the placebo effect</b>. The lecturer also advised going back to the original source.","w":["They do occur.","Placebo is beneficial.","They occur in controlled trials too.","Choice increases them but is not required."],"k":"Trials must control for placebo improvement."},{"id":5044,"t":"Value and Limits of the Biopsychosocial Model","s":"Which of the following is a <b>strength</b> of the biopsychosocial model?","o":["It enables patient-centred care and practitioner reflexivity","It may be regarded as “soft science”","It can place responsibility for health on individuals","It may lead to over-diagnosis of psychosomatic conditions","Its coverage in the curriculum varies across conditions"],"e":"Strengths: sets the patient in their wider context (<b>holistic</b>); affords <b>empathetic practice</b> and a <b>communication toolkit</b>; enables <b>patient-centred care and practitioner reflexivity</b>; enables practitioners to <b>tailor</b> their approach and advice. The other options are limitations.","w":["A limitation.","A limitation.","A limitation.","A limitation."],"k":"Strengths: holistic, empathetic, patient-centred, reflexive, tailored.","rn":[["","What are the 4 strengths of the biopsychosocial model?","What are the 4 strengths of the biopsychosocial model?","Holistic: patient in wider context; Empathetic practice and communication toolkit; Patient-centred care and reflexivity; Tailored advice"]]},{"id":5045,"t":"Value and Limits of the Biopsychosocial Model","s":"A critic says biopsychosocial research mainly uses qualitative methods, which are less rigorous than quantitative ones. Which limitation does this match?","o":["Qualitative methods are seen as inferior to quantitative","It can place responsibility for health on individual patients","It may over-diagnose conditions as psychosomatic","Its curriculum coverage varies between conditions","It sets the patient in their wider social context"],"e":"Limitation: <b>qualitative methodology</b>, dominant in biopsychosocial research, is <b>sometimes regarded as inferior to quantitative</b> methodology. Related: it may be seen as scientifically feeble (“soft science”).","w":["Different limitation.","Different limitation.","Different limitation.","That is a strength."],"k":"Limitation: qualitative seen as inferior.","rn":[["","Why is qualitative research seen as weaker?","Why is qualitative research seen as weaker?","Seen as inferior to quantitative methods"]]},{"id":5046,"t":"Value and Limits of the Biopsychosocial Model","s":"A patient is told they are “not getting better because of factors related to you, not the disease”. Which limitation of the biopsychosocial model does this illustrate?","o":["It can place responsibility for health on individuals","It may be regarded as scientifically feeble","It may over-diagnose conditions as psychosomatic","Its qualitative methods are seen as inferior","Its curriculum coverage varies"],"e":"The model <b>can place responsibility for ‘health’ on individuals</b>: “you're not getting well because of factors related to you, not factors related to the disease.”","w":["Different limitation.","The scenario blames the patient, not the mind.","Different limitation.","Different limitation."],"k":"Risk of individual blame.","rn":[["","Why might the biopsychosocial model place blame on individuals?","Why might the biopsychosocial model place blame on individuals?","Suggests ill health relates to the person; Not just to the disease"]]},{"id":5047,"t":"Value and Limits of the Biopsychosocial Model","s":"In which areas of medicine is biopsychosocial content very low and very high in curricula?","o":["Very low in renal medicine; very high in cardiovascular disease","Very low in cardiovascular disease; very high in renal medicine","Very low in renal medicine; very high in mental health","Very low in mental health; very high in cardiovascular disease","Very low in surgery; very high in renal medicine"],"e":"The incorporation of biopsychosocial topics <b>varies across clinical conditions</b>: it is <b>very low in renal medicine</b> and <b>hugely high in cardiovascular disease</b>, where social and dietary components are known.","w":["Reversed.","The high example was CVD.","The low example was renal.","Not the examples."],"k":"Curriculum coverage: low renal, high cardiovascular.","rn":[["","Where is biopsychosocial content low vs high in the curriculum?","Where is biopsychosocial content low vs high in the curriculum?","Low: renal medicine; High: cardiovascular disease"]]},{"id":5048,"t":"Value and Limits of the Biopsychosocial Model","s":"A real bodily condition is wrongly attributed to the mind. What term describes this over-diagnosis, a limitation of the biopsychosocial model?","e":"Over-diagnosing conditions as psychosomatic is a limitation of the biopsychosocial model.","k":"Psychosomatic.","a":["psychosomatic","psychosomatic diagnosis"],"rn":[["","What are the 5 limitations of the biopsychosocial model?","What are the 5 limitations of the biopsychosocial model?","Puts responsibility for health on individuals; Seen as \"soft science\"; Qualitative methods seen as inferior; Uneven coverage in the curriculum; Over-diagnosis as psychosomatic"],["","What does \"over-diagnosing as psychosomatic\" mean?","What does \"over-diagnosing as psychosomatic\" mean?","Calling a condition mental, not in the body; Because biology and psychology are linked"]]},{"id":5049,"t":"Value and Limits of the Biopsychosocial Model","s":"What practical challenge limits patient-centred, tailored care?","o":["Hard to do for every patient in a time- and cash-limited system","Hard to do because most patients prefer standardised, protocol-driven care","It is hard to do because it removes the need for diagnosis","It is hard to do because it works only for acute disease","It is hard to do because practitioners cannot be reflexive"],"e":"Treating the patient as an individual and tailoring advice is a strength, but it can be <b>difficult for every patient</b> in a system that is <b>time and cash limited</b>.","w":["Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Reflexivity is a strength."],"k":"Tailored care is limited by time and money.","rn":[["","Why is tailored advice hard to give every patient?","Why is tailored advice hard to give every patient?","Systems are time and cash limited"],["","Why is it hard to apply for every patient?","Why is it hard to apply for every patient?","The system is time and cash limited"]]},{"id":5050,"t":"Value and Limits of the Biopsychosocial Model","s":"Which pair correctly matches one strength and one limitation of the biopsychosocial model?","o":["Strength: communication toolkit. Limitation: seen as scientifically feeble","Strength: scientifically rigorous. Limitation: no communication toolkit","Strength: patient-centred care. Limitation: specific aetiology","Strength: reflexivity. Limitation: holistic view of the patient","Strength: places responsibility on individuals. Limitation: soft science"],"e":"Strength: affords empathetic practice and a <b>toolkit for improving communication</b>. Limitation: might be regarded as <b>scientifically feeble (“soft science”)</b>, because it considers “woolly” concepts such as the social environment.","w":["It is seen as soft science.","Specific aetiology is biomedical.","Holism is a strength.","Placing responsibility on individuals is a limitation."],"k":"Pair strengths and limitations correctly.","rn":[["","What are the 4 strengths of the biopsychosocial model?","What are the 4 strengths of the biopsychosocial model?","Holistic: patient in wider context; Empathetic practice and communication toolkit; Patient-centred care and reflexivity; Tailored advice"]]},{"id":5051,"t":"Biology and Society","s":"Which timeline of genetics milestones is correct?","o":["1865 Mendel’s laws; 1942 Modern Synthesis; 2000 genome draft","1865 Darwin’s laws; 1942 Modern Synthesis; 2000 genome draft","1865 Mendel’s laws; 1950 Modern Synthesis; 2000 genome draft","1865 Mendel’s laws; 1942 Modern Synthesis; 1997 genome draft","1942 Mendel’s laws; 1865 Modern Synthesis; 2000 genome draft"],"e":"<b>1865</b>: Mendel established the laws of heredity. <b>1942</b>: the Modern Synthesis. <b>2000</b>: completion of the first draft of the Human Genome Map, hailed “<b>The Century of Genetics</b>” (Duster, 2015).","w":["Mendel established the laws.","Modern Synthesis was 1942.","The genome draft was 2000.","Dates swapped."],"k":"1865 Mendel → 1942 Modern Synthesis → 2000 genome draft.","rn":[["","What happened in 2000?","What happened in 2000?","First draft of the Human Genome Map (\"The Century of Genetics\")"],["","What happened in 1942?","What happened in 1942?","The Modern Synthesis"],["","What happened in 1865?","What happened in 1865?","Mendel established the laws of heredity"]]},{"id":5052,"t":"Biology and Society","s":"What was the relationship between Mendel’s work and Darwin?","o":["Mendel’s work was unknown to Darwin","Darwin based natural selection directly on Mendel’s laws","Mendel and Darwin worked together on the Modern Synthesis","Darwin disproved Mendel’s laws of heredity","Mendel based his laws on Darwin’s natural selection"],"e":"Mendel's very early genetics work was <b>unknown to Charles Darwin</b>, who was developing his ideas on <b>evolution by natural selection</b>. By 1942 genetics and evolution were brought together in the <b>Modern Synthesis</b>.","w":["Mendel’s work was unknown to him.","Not stated; the synthesis was 1942.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Mendel unknown to Darwin; unified in 1942."},{"id":5053,"t":"Biology and Society","s":"What term is now used for the intersection of biology and the social sciences, replacing the older, more deterministic “sociobiology”?","e":"Sociobiology often viewed genetics as deterministic and reductionist; the intersection is now termed biosocial.","k":"Biosocial.","a":["biosocial"],"rn":[["","Biosocial","Biosocial","Current term for the intersection of biological and social; Replaces \"sociobiology\""]]},{"id":5054,"t":"Biology and Society","s":"Name the historical practice of judging character from the shape of the skull.","e":"Phrenology used head/skull shape to judge character; with eugenics it is part of sociobiology’s controversial history.","k":"Phrenology.","a":["phrenology"],"rn":[["","Phrenology","Phrenology","Judging people by the shape of the skull"]]},{"id":5055,"t":"Biology and Society","f":"ph22","s":"Why should the phrase “nature versus nurture” be avoided?","o":["“Nature versus nurture”: genes and environment interact","“Biopsychosocial”, because it is regarded as soft science","“Nature by nurture”, because it wrongly suggests that genes are fixed","“Sociobiology”, because it is deterministic","“Specific aetiology”, because diseases have many causes"],"e":"“If you take anything away from this lecture, it's <b>never use the term nature versus nurture</b>” – it is <b>meaningless</b>. There is no “versus”; it is an <b>intersection</b>, <b>nature by nurture</b>: there are always influences of both genes and environment.","w":["The phrase to avoid was nature versus nurture.","Nature by nurture is the preferred phrase.","Not the phrase.","Not the phrase."],"k":"Nature BY nurture, never versus.","rn":[["","Why is \"nature versus nurture\" meaningless?","Why is \"nature versus nurture\" meaningless?","There is no versus; Genes and environment always interact: nature BY nurture"]]},{"id":5056,"t":"Biology and Society","s":"Meloni (2014) wrote that “sociology is becoming more open to biological suggestions, just at a time when biology is becoming more social”. Which two research areas show this?","o":["Epigenetics and neuroplasticity","Phrenology and eugenics","Germ theory and specific aetiology","Placebo and nocebo effects","Mendelian genetics and the Modern Synthesis"],"e":"The two areas: <b>epigenetics</b> and <b>neuroplasticity</b>. Biology is recognising that <b>social aspects play a role in how biology is expressed</b>, while sociology, previously sceptical of biological reductionism, is becoming more open.","w":["Controversial history.","Biomedical ideas.","Mind–body effects, not the examples.","Earlier genetics."],"k":"New biologies: epigenetics, neuroplasticity.","rn":[["","Why is biology \"becoming more social\"?","Why is biology \"becoming more social\"?","Epigenetics and neuroplasticity research"],["","What are the 2 \"new biologies\"?","What are the 2 \"new biologies\"?","Epigenetics; Neuroplasticity"]]},{"id":5057,"t":"Epigenetics","s":"What term describes the environment shaping whether, or how much, genes are expressed, without changing the DNA sequence?","e":"Epigenetics: environmental factors (diet, activity, chemicals, stress) cause epigenetic modifications that change gene expression – a bridge between the social and biological sciences.","k":"Epigenetics.","a":["epigenetics","epigenetic","epigenetic modification"],"rn":[["","Epigenetics","Epigenetics","How the environment shapes how much, or whether, genes are expressed"],["","What do epigenetic modifications change?","What do epigenetic modifications change?","Gene expression"]]},{"id":5058,"t":"Epigenetics","s":"Name one epigenetic modification that can change gene expression (e.g. in obesity-related genes).","e":"Epigenetic modifications: DNA methylation, modification of histone tails, and non-coding RNAs.","k":"e.g. DNA methylation.","a":["dna methylation","methylation","histone modification","modification of histone tails","histone tail modification","non-coding rna","non coding rnas","noncoding rna","histone acetylation"],"rn":[["","What are the 3 epigenetic modifications?","What are the 3 epigenetic modifications?","DNA methylation; Histone tail modification; Non-coding RNAs"]]},{"id":5059,"t":"Epigenetics","s":"McGowan and Szyf (2010) described epigenetics as a potential “bridge”. Between what?","o":["The social and biological sciences","Psychology and the biological sciences","Medicine and the social sciences","Genetics and evolutionary biology","Placebo and nocebo research"],"e":"“Epigenetics could serve as a <b>bridge between the social sciences and the biological sciences</b>, allowing a truly integrated understanding of human health and behaviour” (McGowan and Szyf, 2010:71).","w":["It was the social sciences.","Biological sciences.","That was the Modern Synthesis.","Not stated."],"k":"Epigenetics bridges social and biological sciences.","rn":[["","Why might epigenetics matter to social science?","Why might epigenetics matter to social science?","A bridge between social and biological sciences (McGowan and Szyf, 2010)"]]},{"id":5060,"t":"Epigenetics","s":"Which key question about epigenetic changes is central to their importance?","o":["Whether it is passed from parent to offspring","Whether a change alters the DNA sequence","Whether a change can be reversed later in adulthood","Whether a change occurs only in the brain","Whether a change is caused by infection"],"e":"Epigenetics asks how genes change and <b>how changes are passed on</b>. A key question is whether a change is <b>passed on from parent to offspring</b>, which the following studies (Tegethoff, Kuzawa and Sweet, Jasienska) explore.","w":["Expression, not sequence.","Not the key question.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Are epigenetic changes inherited?"},{"id":5061,"t":"Epigenetics","s":"Tegethoff et al. (2010) defined two types of psychosocial stress during pregnancy. Which pair is correct?","o":["Life stress and emotional symptoms","Acute stress and chronic stress","Work stress and financial stress","Life stress and acute work stress","Emotional symptoms and physical symptoms"],"e":"Tegethoff et al. defined <b>life stress</b> (perceived burdens in major areas of life) and <b>emotional symptoms</b> (e.g. anxiety; “how are you doing day to day?”). Other studies have looked at childhood stress; this one looked <b>during pregnancy</b>.","w":["Not the categories.","Not the categories.","Acute work stress was a student question.","Not the categories."],"k":"Tegethoff: life stress vs emotional symptoms.","rn":[["","What 2 types of pregnancy stress did Tegethoff et al. (2010) define?","What 2 types of pregnancy stress did Tegethoff et al. (2010) define?","Life stress (burdens in major life areas); Emotional symptoms (e.g. anxiety)"],["","What did Tegethoff et al. (2010) find?","What did Tegethoff et al. (2010) find?","Maternal life stress, not emotional symptoms; Linked to heavier placenta at birth"]]},{"id":5062,"t":"Epigenetics","s":"What did Tegethoff et al. (2010) find?","o":["Life stress, but not emotional symptoms, was linked to heavier placentas","Emotional symptoms, but not life stress, were linked to heavier placentas","Both types of stress were linked to heavier placentas","Life stress, but not emotional symptoms, was linked to lighter placentas","Life stress, but not emotional symptoms, was linked to heavier babies"],"e":"<b>Maternal life stress, but not emotional symptoms</b>, during pregnancy was associated with <b>increased placental weight at birth</b>. The study used a national birth cohort (1996–2002, about 78,000 births).","w":["Reversed.","Only life stress.","Heavier.","Placental weight."],"k":"Life stress → heavier placenta.","rn":[["","What did Tegethoff et al. (2010) find?","What did Tegethoff et al. (2010) find?","Maternal life stress, not emotional symptoms; Linked to heavier placenta at birth"]]},{"id":5063,"t":"Epigenetics","s":"At birth, a baby has a heavy placenta and a low birth weight. Why is this concerning?","o":["The placenta worked harder for nutrients, yet the baby did not grow","The placenta has stored nutrients that the baby has not needed","A heavy placenta protects the baby against later cardiovascular disease","The placenta has grown because the mother had no life stress","Low birth weight protects the baby from metabolic disease"],"e":"A big placenta is <b>not</b> good: it suggests it is <b>working harder to get nutrients to the fetus</b>. Especially when <b>placental weight is high and birth weight low</b>, the placenta has been trying hard and the baby is not responding. Such an adverse intrauterine environment has been associated with <b>later cardiovascular/metabolic disease and premature mortality</b>.","w":["Not stated.","An adverse intrauterine environment increases risk.","Life stress is linked to heavier placentas.","LBW is a risk."],"k":"Heavy placenta + low birth weight = adverse intrauterine environment.","rn":[["","Why can a large placenta signal a problem?","Why can a large placenta signal a problem?","Working harder to supply nutrients; Baby may not respond"],["","Why is a heavy placenta bad?","Why is a heavy placenta bad?","Working harder to feed the fetus; Esp. with low birth weight"]]},{"id":5064,"t":"Epigenetics","s":"An adverse intrauterine environment has repeatedly been associated with which long-term outcomes?","o":["Later cardiovascular/metabolic disease and premature mortality","Later cardiovascular disease, but not premature mortality","Later mental illness and learning difficulties","Lower birth weight only, with no later effects","Later infectious disease and reduced immunity"],"e":"An adverse intrauterine environment has repeatedly been associated with <b>later cardiovascular/metabolic diseases and premature mortality</b>; it is trackable over time, including in children born <b>after wars and famine</b>.","w":["Premature mortality too.","Not stated.","Effects are long-term.","Not stated."],"k":"Adverse intrauterine environment → later cardiometabolic disease.","rn":[["","An adverse intrauterine environment is linked to what later?","An adverse intrauterine environment is linked to what later?","Cardiovascular / metabolic disease; Premature mortality"]]},{"id":5065,"t":"Epigenetics","s":"Kuzawa and Sweet (2009) examined the gap in cardiovascular health between African American and US White populations. What was their central conclusion?","o":["Biology is the outcome of racialisation, not the basis of race","Race is a biological category that underlies the CVD gap","The CVD gap is explained mainly by genetic differences","The CVD gap is explained mainly by diet, not racism","Racialisation affects behaviour but not biology"],"e":"<b>Race as a social construct</b> can produce <b>real, harmful effects on bodies</b>: <b>biology is the outcome of racialisation rather than the underpinning of race</b>. Stress from racism, not “race” itself, causes the difference, so we should explore social reasons rather than simplistic ethnic explanations.","w":["Reversed.","Not genetic.","Stress from racism.","It affects biology."],"k":"Kuzawa & Sweet: racialisation (stress) → biology.","rn":[["","What did Kuzawa and Sweet (2009) conclude?","What did Kuzawa and Sweet (2009) conclude?","Race as a social construct causes real harm; Biology is the outcome of racialisation, not the basis of race"],["","What did Kuzawa and Sweet (2009) say about race and biology?","What did Kuzawa and Sweet (2009) say about race and biology?","Biology is the outcome of racialisation; Not the underpinning of race"]]},{"id":5066,"t":"Epigenetics","s":"In Kuzawa and Sweet's model, how might prenatal stress affect the next generation?","o":["Via the womb: raised maternal cortisol, insulin and BP","Via changes in the DNA sequence passed on by the father","Via raised maternal cortisol passed on in breast milk","Via shared diet in the family after birth","Via the placenta becoming lighter in each of the later pregnancies"],"e":"Prenatal stress → induced changes in metabolism and physiology → <b>perpetuation to the next generation via the intrauterine environment</b> (<b>↑ maternal cortisol, ↑ insulin, ↑ blood pressure</b>) → further perpetuation, with <b>ongoing stress</b> acting on each generation.","w":["Not sequence change.","Via the intrauterine environment.","Not the mechanism given.","Not the mechanism."],"k":"Maternal cortisol/insulin/BP carry stress effects to the fetus.","rn":[["","How can prenatal stress affect the next generation?","How can prenatal stress affect the next generation?","Raised maternal cortisol, insulin and BP; Change fetal metabolism and physiology"],["","What changes may prenatal stress pass to the next generation?","What changes may prenatal stress pass to the next generation?","Raised cortisol, insulin, blood pressure; Via the intrauterine environment"]]},{"id":5067,"t":"Epigenetics","s":"A colleague argues that higher CVD rates in a minority group must mean that “this race has these conditions”. What is the best response?","o":["Look past simple explanations to social causes such as racism","Agree, as race is a biological category with its own diseases","Agree, as each disease has a single specific cause","Explain it mainly by differences in diet","Explain it as a placebo effect of expectations"],"e":"The lecturer concluded the biological difference is <b>not</b> because “this race has these conditions”; it is <b>racialisation (the stress)</b> causing the difference. Step away from simplistic explanations of disease differences between ethnic groups and explore the <b>social reasons</b>.","w":["Race is not the biological cause.","Specific aetiology is biomedical.","Not the lecturer’s response.","Not relevant."],"k":"Look for social causes behind ethnic differences.","rn":[["","How was racism linked to disease in Kuzawa and Sweet (2009)?","How was racism linked to disease in Kuzawa and Sweet (2009)?","Racism raises stress; Race as social construct harms bodies"],["","What did Kuzawa and Sweet (2009) conclude?","What did Kuzawa and Sweet (2009) conclude?","Race as a social construct causes real harm; Biology is the outcome of racialisation, not the basis of race"]]},{"id":5068,"t":"Epigenetics","s":"Two pregnant women: A has severe, ongoing burdens at work and home but reports feeling fine day to day; B reports anxiety but no major life burdens. Based on Tegethoff et al. (2010), which placenta is more likely to be heavier at birth?","o":["A’s, because life stress was linked to placental weight","B’s, because emotional symptoms were linked to placental weight","Both equally, as any stress raises placental weight","Neither, as stress affects only birth weight","B’s, because anxiety lowers maternal cortisol"],"e":"Tegethoff et al. defined life stress (perceived burdens in major areas of life) and emotional symptoms (e.g. anxiety). Maternal life stress, but not emotional symptoms, during pregnancy was associated with increased placental weight – a sign the placenta was working harder.","w":["Emotional symptoms were NOT associated with placental weight.","Only life stress showed the association.","The finding concerned placental weight.","Not stated, and emotional symptoms showed no association."],"k":"Life stress, not anxiety, → heavier placenta.","rn":[["","What did Tegethoff et al. (2010) find?","What did Tegethoff et al. (2010) find?","Maternal life stress, not emotional symptoms; Linked to heavier placenta at birth"],["","What 2 types of pregnancy stress did Tegethoff et al. (2010) define?","What 2 types of pregnancy stress did Tegethoff et al. (2010) define?","Life stress (burdens in major life areas); Emotional symptoms (e.g. anxiety)"]]},{"id":5069,"t":"Epigenetics","s":"According to Jasienska (2009), how likely are African American women to deliver babies under 2.5 kg compared with European American women?","o":["Twice as likely","Half as likely","Equally likely","Ten times as likely","Three times less likely"],"e":"African American women are ‘<b>twice as likely as European American women to deliver children with weight below 2.5 kg</b>’ (low birth weight). Jasienska looked to a specific historical event, slavery, as a cause.","w":["Opposite.","There is a difference.","Not the figure.","Opposite."],"k":"Jasienska: 2× risk of LBW (<2.5 kg).","rn":[["","How much more likely are African American women to have babies under 2.5 kg?","How much more likely are African American women to have babies under 2.5 kg?","Twice as likely as European American women"]]},{"id":5070,"t":"Epigenetics","s":"Which evidence best supports the slavery hypothesis?","o":["African-born black women in the US have heavier babies than US black women of slave ancestry","US black women of slave ancestry have heavier babies than African-born black women in the US","African-born and US-born black women in the US have babies of equal weight","European American women have the lowest-birth-weight babies in the US","Birth weight among US black women is unrelated to ancestry"],"e":"“Contemporary black women who were <b>born in African countries ancestral to slave populations</b>, but who live in the United States, give birth to children with <b>significantly higher weight</b> than black women in the United States who <b>have slave ancestry</b>” (2009:16). This separates slave ancestry from genetic background.","w":["Reversed.","There was a significant difference.","Not stated.","Ancestry was relevant."],"k":"Key comparison: African-born vs US-born with slave ancestry.","rn":[["","What is the key evidence for the slavery hypothesis?","What is the key evidence for the slavery hypothesis?","African-born black women in the US have heavier babies; Than US black women with slave ancestry"]]},{"id":5071,"t":"Epigenetics","s":"Why did Jasienska consider a link between slavery and low birth weight at the time of slavery plausible?","o":["Maternal undernutrition and intense physical labour","Maternal undernutrition and infectious disease","Intense physical labour and genetic mutations","Psychological stress and better nutrition","Infectious disease and intense physical labour"],"e":"A connection between slavery and LBW at the time seems plausible: <b>maternal undernutrition and intense physical labour</b> are obviously potential causes for smaller babies (2009:22). She argued <b>life conditions in the slavery period are responsible for LBW of African Americans today</b>.","w":["Labour, not infection.","Not genetic.","Not better nutrition.","Undernutrition."],"k":"Undernutrition + labour → smaller babies.","rn":[["","Which slavery-era conditions could cause smaller babies?","Which slavery-era conditions could cause smaller babies?","Maternal undernutrition; Intense physical labour"]]},{"id":5072,"t":"Epigenetics","s":"What is the significance of slavery having been abolished in 1865 for Jasienska's argument?","o":["Generations since have not removed its impact on biology","It shows slavery’s impact on birth weight ended soon after abolition","It shows LBW in this group began only after 1865","It shows genetics alone explain the LBW gap","It marks when Mendel published the laws of heredity"],"e":"As paraphrased in the lecture, several generations have passed since abolition (<b>1865</b>), but this <b>has not been enough to obliterate the impact of slavery</b> on the current biological and health conditions of African Americans, including birth weight and potentially other metabolic diseases. (1865 is also Mendel's date – a coincidence.)","w":["The impact persists.","Not stated.","Not genetic.","1865 is also Mendel, but not the significance here."],"k":"Generations later, slavery's effect persists.","rn":[["","Why might slavery still affect birth weight today?","Why might slavery still affect birth weight today?","Several generations not enough; To remove its biological impact"]]},{"id":5073,"t":"Pain, Placebo and Nocebo","s":"Which UK example parallels intergenerational effects of hardship on health?","o":["Pit-mining villages in Wales with high CVD today (linked to Barker)","Pit-mining villages in Wales with high LBW today (linked to Engel)","Coastal towns in Scotland with high CVD today (linked to Barker)","Industrial towns in the north with high diabetes (linked to Kirsch)","London boroughs with high CVD today (linked to Maguire)"],"e":"She thought it was <b>David Barker (Southampton)</b>: areas of the UK that were incredibly impoverished, such as <b>pit-mining villages in Wales</b>, still have very high rates of <b>cardiovascular disease</b>, where there was real poverty and hard physical labour.","w":["CVD, Barker.","Not the example.","Not the example.","Maguire studied taxi drivers."],"k":"UK parallel: Welsh mining villages and CVD.","rn":[["","Why can impoverished past conditions still matter?","Why can impoverished past conditions still matter?","Hard labour and poverty linked to high CVD (cardiovascular disease) rates today in UK areas"]]},{"id":5074,"t":"Epigenetics","s":"Match each study to its focus. Which is correct?","o":["Tegethoff – placenta; Kuzawa and Sweet – race and CVD; Jasienska – slavery and LBW","Tegethoff – slavery and LBW; Kuzawa and Sweet – race and CVD; Jasienska – placenta","Tegethoff – placenta; Kuzawa and Sweet – slavery and LBW; Jasienska – race and CVD","Tegethoff – race and CVD; Kuzawa and Sweet – placenta; Jasienska – slavery and LBW","Tegethoff – placenta; Kuzawa and Sweet – taxi drivers; Jasienska – slavery and LBW"],"e":"Tegethoff et al. (2010) – <b>maternal stress and placental weight</b>; Kuzawa and Sweet (2009) – <b>racialisation and CVD</b>; Jasienska (2009) – <b>slavery and LBW</b>. Memory aid: “The Key Journey”.","w":["Tegethoff studied placental weight.","Kuzawa and Sweet studied race and CVD.","Tegethoff studied placental weight.","Maguire studied taxi drivers."],"k":"T: placenta; K&S: race/CVD; J: slavery/LBW.","rn":[["","What did Kuzawa and Sweet (2009) say about race and biology?","What did Kuzawa and Sweet (2009) say about race and biology?","Biology is the outcome of racialisation; Not the underpinning of race"],["","What did Kuzawa and Sweet (2009) study?","What did Kuzawa and Sweet (2009) study?","The CVD gap between African American and US White populations"],["","What did Kuzawa and Sweet (2009) conclude?","What did Kuzawa and Sweet (2009) conclude?","Race as a social construct causes real harm; Biology is the outcome of racialisation, not the basis of race"]]},{"id":5075,"t":"Neuroplasticity","f":"ph29","s":"A woman scanned for an unrelated reason has a fluid-filled gap in her left temporal lobe yet is cognitively normal. What most likely happened?","o":["A likely infant stroke, with language moving to the right side","A likely infant stroke, with language lost but compensated by reading","A congenital absence of the temporal lobe since conception","A recent stroke, with language moving to the frontal lobe","A likely infant stroke, with language moving to the cerebellum"],"e":"She likely had an <b>undiagnosed stroke as an infant</b>; her <b>language processing</b> seems to have moved from the left to the <b>right side</b> of the brain. She came across as completely cognitively normal: her brain had <b>rewired</b> – neuroplasticity.","w":["She was cognitively normal.","Likely infant stroke.","Left to right.","Left to right hemisphere."],"k":"Infant stroke case: language rewired to the right.","rn":[["","What happened in the brain scan case?","What happened in the brain scan case?","Left temporal lobe missing (likely infant stroke); Language moved to the right side; She was cognitively normal"],["","Why did a woman with missing left temporal lobe seem normal?","Why did a woman with missing left temporal lobe seem normal?","Language moved to the right side; Likely infant stroke, undiagnosed"]]},{"id":5076,"t":"Neuroplasticity","s":"A woman whose language processing moved to the right hemisphere after an infant stroke is cognitively normal. What single term describes the brain’s ability to rewire like this?","e":"Neuroplasticity: the brain is malleable; new connections form and old ones can be rewired, even in adults.","k":"Neuroplasticity.","a":["neuroplasticity","plasticity","neural plasticity","brain plasticity"],"rn":[["","Neuroplasticity","Neuroplasticity","The brain can be re-organised or rewired"],["","What are the 2 \"new biologies\"?","What are the 2 \"new biologies\"?","Epigenetics; Neuroplasticity"]]},{"id":5077,"t":"Neuroplasticity","s":"Which brain structure was enlarged in experienced London taxi drivers?","e":"Maguire et al. (2006): MRI showed substantial enlargement of the hippocampi, correlating with years of driving.","k":"Hippocampus.","a":["hippocampus","hippocampi","hippocampal"],"rn":[["","What does the hippocampus link to in taxi drivers?","What does the hippocampus link to in taxi drivers?","Long-term memory and spatial cognition; Memory and recall"]]},{"id":5078,"t":"Neuroplasticity","s":"Why is the taxi-driver study particularly important?","o":["It shows the adult brain changes, aiding dementia research","It shows structural change happens only in the developing child brain","It shows taxi driving causes dementia in later life","It shows nature determines brain structure","It shows the brain cannot change after injury"],"e":"This happens <b>even in the adult brain</b> (drivers must be adults). It <b>contributes to dementia research</b> and to understanding how adult brains may be malleable and respond to stimuli.","w":["Drivers are adults.","Not stated.","Nature by nurture.","Not relevant."],"k":"Adult plasticity; relevance to dementia.","rn":[["","Why do adult taxi drivers' brain changes matter?","Why do adult taxi drivers' brain changes matter?","Adult brains are malleable; Contributes to dementia research"]]},{"id":5079,"t":"Pain, Placebo and Nocebo","s":"A 58-year-old lorry driver with chest pain tells his GP he “can’t afford time off” and has ignored symptoms for months. His wife says his father died young of heart disease. Which set of factors fits each biopsychosocial domain?","o":["Bio: family history; psych: minimising symptoms; social: job/economic pressure","Bio: job pressure; psych: family history; social: minimising symptoms","Bio: family history; psych: job pressure; social: minimising symptoms","Bio: minimising symptoms; psych: family history; social: job pressure","All three are biological risk factors for CHD"],"e":"Genetic vulnerability (family history of early heart disease) is biological; attitudes, beliefs and coping (minimising symptoms) are psychological; socioeconomic status and work pressures are social. Men are also less likely to consult generally, which shapes when disease is detected.","w":["Job pressure is a social/economic factor and a family history is biological.","Job pressure is economic (social); minimising symptoms is a psychological response.","Ignoring symptoms is psychological/behavioural, not biological.","Only the family history (genetic vulnerability) is biological."],"k":"Sort factors into bio, psych and social domains.","rn":[["","What are the social factors in pain?","What are the social factors in pain?","Cultural factors; Social environment; Economic factors; Social support"],["","What does the bio + psych overlap show about mind and body?","What does the bio + psych overlap show about mind and body?","Mind-body monism, not dualism"],["","What 7 types of factor shape health status?","What 7 types of factor shape health status?","Social; Cultural; Historical; Economic; Psychological; Biological; Environmental; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":5080,"t":"Social Factors in Disease","s":"A woman describes tiredness and nausea with exertion. Her ECG is reported as normal and she is reassured. Weeks later she is diagnosed with coronary heart disease. Which social explanation for the delay is best supported?","o":["An assumption that women are at lower risk, or symptoms described atypically","Women consult less often than men, so their disease is found later","Women’s CHD is caused by a different pathogen from men’s","The placebo effect of reassurance hid her symptoms","Her symptoms were a nocebo response to the ECG"],"e":"Women are less likely to be diagnosed with CHD than men, even though men consult less. Possible social reasons: an assumption that women are less at risk, or women describing symptoms in a way that does not match the expected symptoms of CHD. Diagnosis is socially located.","w":["Men are less likely to consult generally, not women.","CHD is not explained by a specific pathogen here.","Placebo explains improvement, not missed diagnosis.","Nocebo is harm from belief in harm."],"k":"Under-diagnosis of CHD in women is social.","rn":[["","Why might women be under-diagnosed with CHD?","Why might women be under-diagnosed with CHD?","Assumption women are less at risk; Symptoms described differently from the expected pattern"]]},{"id":5081,"t":"The Biopsychosocial Model","s":"A patient with chronic pain catastrophises, has little social support and has severe inflammation. Which approach best accounts for all three factors?","o":["The biopsychosocial model of pain","The biomedical model of pain","The doctrine of specific aetiology","Mind/body dualism","The mechanical metaphor"],"e":"Catastrophising is <b>psychological</b>, social support is <b>social</b>, and inflammation is <b>biological</b>. The <b>biopsychosocial model of pain</b> places PAIN in the centre with all three, whereas the biomedical model is reductionist.","w":["Misses psychological and social factors.","One cause per disease.","Separates mind and body.","Body as a machine."],"k":"Pain needs the biopsychosocial model.","rn":[["","What sits at the centre of the biopsychosocial model?","What sits at the centre of the biopsychosocial model?","Well-being"],["","What are the 3 circles of the biopsychosocial model?","What are the 3 circles of the biopsychosocial model?","Biological; Psychological; Sociological"]]},{"id":5082,"t":"The Biomedical Model","s":"Which statement best contrasts the biomedical and biopsychosocial models?","o":["Biomedical: reductionist and dualist; biopsychosocial: holistic, mind linked to body","Biomedical: holistic and dualist; biopsychosocial: reductionist, mind linked to body","Biomedical: reductionist and holistic; biopsychosocial: dualist, mind separate from body","Biomedical: reductionist, mind linked to body; biopsychosocial: holistic and dualist","Both reductionist; biopsychosocial adds social factors only"],"e":"The biomedical model is <b>reductionist</b> and treats mind and body separately (<b>dualism</b>). The biopsychosocial model is <b>holistic</b> and treats psychology and biology as linked (<b>monism</b>).","w":["Biomedical is reductionist.","Biopsychosocial links mind and body.","Biomedical is dualist.","Biopsychosocial is holistic."],"k":"Biomedical: reductionist/dualist. BPS: holistic/monist.","rn":[["","What are Nettleton's (2021) 5 assumptions of the biomedical model?","What are Nettleton's (2021) 5 assumptions of the biomedical model?","Mind/body dualism; Mechanical metaphor (body can be repaired); Prioritises technological responses; Reductionist (biological focus); Doctrine of specific aetiology; MNEMONIC"],["","Mind/body dualism","Mind/body dualism","Treating mind and body as separate"]]},{"id":5083,"t":"Social Factors in Disease","s":"Which of the following examples shows social factors affecting the <b>outcome</b> of a disease rather than its diagnosis?","o":["Higher Covid-19 death rates in vaccine-suspicious groups","Under-diagnosis of coronary heart disease in women","Freud’s concept of female hysteria","Drapetomania in the pre-Civil War US","ADHD becoming a validating label"],"e":"Vaccine suspicion led to <b>higher death rates</b> – an <b>outcome</b>. Hysteria, drapetomania and CHD in women concern <b>diagnosis</b>; ADHD labels concern experience.","w":["Diagnosis.","Diagnosis.","Diagnosis.","Experience/labels."],"k":"Distinguish diagnosis vs outcome vs experience.","rn":[["","What did vaccine suspicion do in the US during Covid-19?","What did vaccine suspicion do in the US during Covid-19?","Much higher death rates in vaccine-suspicious groups"]]},{"id":5084,"t":"Social Factors in Disease","s":"Which example best shows that the meaning of a disease can change how it is treated by society?","o":["AIDS protest placards: “Fight AIDS not gays”","Ebola burial practices and hidden bodies","Covid-19 vaccine suspicion in the US","Rural Nepal and back pain in old age","ADHD sought as a validating diagnosis"],"e":"The slide shows protest placards (“Fight AIDS not gays”, “Bigotry”, “Fear”), illustrating the <b>social/cultural meaning</b> of AIDS impacting experience and treatment.","w":["That is about transmission.","That is about outcomes.","That is about seeking care.","That is about labels, not societal treatment."],"k":"AIDS imagery: social meaning of disease."},{"id":5085,"t":"Value and Limits of the Biopsychosocial Model","s":"A GP adapts advice about diet for a patient who works night shifts and cares for relatives. Which strength of the biopsychosocial model is this?","o":["Tailoring the approach and advice to the individual","Setting the patient in their wider social context","Providing a toolkit for improving communication","Practitioner reflexivity","Empathetic practice"],"e":"The model <b>enables medical practitioners to tailor their approach and advice</b> to the individual's circumstances, and supports patient-centred care.","w":["Related, but the action is tailoring.","Not the main point.","Related, but tailoring is the point.","Related, but tailoring is the point."],"k":"Tailored advice = BPS strength.","rn":[["","What are the 4 strengths of the biopsychosocial model?","What are the 4 strengths of the biopsychosocial model?","Holistic: patient in wider context; Empathetic practice and communication toolkit; Patient-centred care and reflexivity; Tailored advice"],["","Why is tailored advice hard to give every patient?","Why is tailored advice hard to give every patient?","Systems are time and cash limited"]]},{"id":5086,"t":"Pain, Placebo and Nocebo","s":"Which pairing of researcher and finding is correct?","o":["Beecher (1955) – placebo effect in 32% of patients","Beecher (1955) – placebo effect in 50–60% of patients","Kirsch – placebo greater when patients choose treatment","Rose et al. (2012) – Prozac largely placebo in mild depression","Maguire et al. (2006) – placental weight and life stress"],"e":"Beecher (1955): placebo in 32%. Kirsch: Prozac in mild depression largely placebo. Rose et al. (2012): choice increases placebo. Maguire et al. (2006): taxi drivers. Nettleton (2021): five biomedical assumptions.","w":["Later research found 50–60%.","That is Rose et al.","That is Kirsch.","Maguire studied taxi drivers."],"k":"Match researchers to findings.","rn":[["","What % had a placebo effect in early studies?","What % had a placebo effect in early studies?","32% (Beecher, 1955)"],["","How common is the placebo effect?","How common is the placebo effect?","32% (Beecher, 1955); Later research: 50-60%"]]},{"id":5087,"t":"Neuroplasticity","s":"A patient in a high-stress job responds poorly to treatment. Which summary point does this best illustrate?","o":["Be cognisant of the wider context of the patient’s life","Understandings of health, illness, sickness and disease are contested","The biomedical model has huge value but misses social factors","Biology is the outcome of racialisation","The brain can be rewired by experience"],"e":"Example from the summary: a <b>stressful job</b> may affect how a patient responds to treatment, or why they come to you in the first place. Be <b>cognisant of the wider context</b>.","w":["True, but less specific to this scenario.","True, but less specific.","Not the scenario.","Not the scenario."],"k":"Context (e.g. job stress) shapes treatment response.","rn":[["","What are the 4 summary points of the lecture?","What are the 4 summary points of the lecture?","Health, illness and disease are contested; Know the patient's wider context; Biopsychosocial model enables reflexive, patient-centred practice; Social and psychological influences offer opportunities for medicine"],["","What are the 4 strengths of the biopsychosocial model?","What are the 4 strengths of the biopsychosocial model?","Holistic: patient in wider context; Empathetic practice and communication toolkit; Patient-centred care and reflexivity; Tailored advice"]]},{"id":5088,"t":"Biology and Society","s":"Name the ideology that only people with the “best genetics” should reproduce, which fed into Nazi ideology.","e":"Eugenics held that only certain people should reproduce; it fed into Nazi ideology and is part of why the field was renamed biosocial.","k":"Eugenics.","a":["eugenics"],"rn":[["","What is eugenics linked to historically?","What is eugenics linked to historically?","Nazi ideology"],["","Eugenics","Eugenics","Idea that only people with the \"best genetics\" should reproduce"]]},{"id":5089,"t":"Neuroplasticity","s":"An adult who learns a demanding new spatial skill over many years shows a structural brain change on MRI. Which finding would best match Maguire et al. (2006)?","o":["Enlarged hippocampi, related to years of practice","Shrunken hippocampi, related to years of practice","No change, as adult brains cannot change","Enlarged hippocampi, unrelated to experience","Loss of the left temporal lobe"],"e":"London cab drivers (who must learn “the Knowledge”) showed substantial hippocampal enlargement on MRI, correlating with years of experience – evidence of adult neuroplasticity, relevant to dementia research.","w":["The finding was enlargement.","The study showed adult brains do change.","The change correlated with years of experience.","That was a separate case of infant stroke."],"k":"Taxi drivers: bigger hippocampi with experience.","rn":[["","What did Maguire et al. (2006) find in London taxi drivers?","What did Maguire et al. (2006) find in London taxi drivers?","Substantially enlarged hippocampi"]]},{"id":5090,"t":"Social Factors in Disease","s":"During an outbreak, families stop reporting deaths because relief workers remove bodies before burial rites, and the infection spreads further. Which conclusion is best supported?","o":["The cultural meaning of a disease shapes its spread and control","The outbreak shows the doctrine of specific aetiology is wrong","Families experienced a placebo effect from burial rites","The spread was caused only by the virus’s biology","Burial rites are a nocebo response"],"e":"In the Ebola outbreak, bodies were not returned to families because of transmission risk, but preparing the dead for burial was very important. Families began withholding bodies and not reporting deaths out of distrust, which spread Ebola further. The social meaning of a disease affects its experience and treatment.","w":["A specific virus can still cause the disease; the social response altered transmission.","Placebo is improvement not due to treatment.","Ignoring the social response misses why spread increased.","Nocebo is harm from expecting harm."],"k":"Social meaning and trust affect disease control.","rn":[["","How did cultural meaning worsen Ebola?","How did cultural meaning worsen Ebola?","Bodies not returned for burial preparation; Families hid bodies and did not report deaths; Distrust of Red Cross workers spread disease"]]},{"id":5091,"t":"The Biomedical Model","s":"Which statement about the five-assumption list is accurate?","o":["Reductionism focuses on biology over other influences","Reductionism includes social and psychological factors","The mechanical metaphor treats the mind as the machine","Specific aetiology means each disease has many causes","Technological responses are a limitation the biomedical model accepts"],"e":"Assumption 4: focuses on the biological – <b>reductionist at the expense of other influences</b>, just focusing on the cause of the disease and how to fix it.","w":["It excludes them.","The body is the machine.","One cause per disease.","The model prioritises them."],"k":"Reductionism ignores non-biological influences.","rn":[["","Reductionist","Reductionist","Focusing only on the biological at the expense of other influences"]]},{"id":5092,"t":"Epigenetics","s":"A mother experiences a severe ongoing burden at work and home during pregnancy but reports no anxiety. Based on Tegethoff et al. Which outcome is most expected?","o":["Increased placental weight at birth","Reduced placental weight","No association, since she reports no anxiety","Enlarged hippocampus in the baby","Increased birth weight"],"e":"An ongoing burden in major areas of life is <b>life stress</b>. Tegethoff found <b>life stress, but not emotional symptoms</b>, was associated with <b>increased placental weight</b>. Absence of anxiety does not remove the life-stress association.","w":["Opposite.","Life stress was the associated factor.","Taxi drivers.","Not stated."],"k":"Life stress (not anxiety) → heavier placenta.","rn":[["","What is \"LBW\" (low birth weight)?","What is \"LBW\" (low birth weight)?","Weight below 2.5 kg"],["","Why is a heavy placenta bad?","Why is a heavy placenta bad?","Working harder to feed the fetus; Esp. with low birth weight"],["","Why might slavery still affect birth weight today?","Why might slavery still affect birth weight today?","Several generations not enough; To remove its biological impact"]]},{"id":5093,"t":"Social Factors in Disease","s":"Name the historical “female condition” that Freud described in women not coping with family life or marriage.","e":"Freud’s female hysteria shows diagnosis is historically located; today such women might be recognised as having e.g. postnatal depression.","k":"Hysteria.","a":["hysteria","female hysteria"],"rn":[["","Hysteria","Hysteria","Freud's \"female condition\"; Today might be seen as e.g. postnatal depression"],["","What was hysteria (Freud) thought to be?","What was hysteria (Freud) thought to be?","A \"female condition\"; Women not coping with family life or marriage"]]},{"id":5094,"t":"Value and Limits of the Biopsychosocial Model","s":"Why might the biopsychosocial model be seen as “soft science”?","o":["It includes “woolly” concepts like the social environment","It ignores biology and genetics altogether in favour of society","It relies mainly on technological responses","It relies mainly on quantitative methods","It focuses mainly on specific aetiology"],"e":"It does not rely only on biology, genetics and underlying biological causes; it takes into account “<b>more woolly</b> concepts” such as the social environment, so it may be regarded as <b>scientifically feeble</b>.","w":["It includes biology.","That is biomedical.","Qualitative.","That is biomedical."],"k":"“Soft science” critique.","rn":[["","Why is the model called \"soft science\"?","Why is the model called \"soft science\"?","Considers vague concepts like social environment; Not only biology, genetics, biological causes"]]},{"id":5095,"t":"The Biomedical Model","s":"Which statement about the number of deaths in England and Wales in 1900, 1950 and 1997 is correct?","o":["1900 had the most (587,830) and 1950 the fewest (510,526)","1997 had the most (558,052) and 1950 the fewest (510,526)","1900 had the most (587,830) and 1997 the fewest (558,052)","1950 had the most (510,526) and 1900 the fewest (587,830)","All three years had roughly 510,000 deaths"],"e":"1900: <b>587,830</b>; 1950: <b>510,526</b>; 1997: <b>558,052</b>. The numbers do not change dramatically; the causes do.","w":["1900 had the most.","1950 had the fewest.","Not correct.","Not correct."],"k":"Death totals 1900/1950/1997.","rn":[["","Deaths in England and Wales: 1900 vs 1950 vs 1997","Deaths in England and Wales: 1900 vs 1950 vs 1997","1900: 587,830; 1950: 520,526; 1997: 558,052"]]},{"id":5096,"t":"Pain, Placebo and Nocebo","s":"A patient believes a new medication is poisoning them and develops symptoms that match this belief, although tests show nothing. Another patient improves on a sugar pill they chose themselves. Which pair of effects is shown?","o":["Nocebo, then placebo (enhanced by choice)","Placebo, then nocebo (enhanced by choice)","Nocebo, then nocebo","Placebo, then placebo (enhanced by choice)","Nocebo, then specific aetiology"],"e":"Symptoms matching anticipated harm from an inert cause = <b>nocebo</b>. Improvement not attributable to treatment, greater when people <b>choose</b> treatment = <b>placebo</b> (Rose et al., 2012).","w":["Reversed.","The second is placebo.","The first is nocebo.","Not specific aetiology."],"k":"Nocebo = harm; placebo = improvement.","rn":[["","How do you remember placebo vs nocebo?","How do you remember placebo vs nocebo?","Placebo = \"I will please\"; Nocebo = \"I will harm\""],["","What do placebo and nocebo show about pain and recovery?","What do placebo and nocebo show about pain and recovery?","Psychological and social factors feed into both"]]},{"id":5097,"t":"Health, Illness, Disease and Sickness","s":"Which statement about lay conceptions of health and the WHO definition is correct?","o":["Lay: no disease, no illness, fitness; WHO adds mental and social","Lay: absence of disease only; WHO adds illness and functional fitness","Lay: functional fitness only; WHO adds absence of disease","Lay and WHO definitions are effectively identical","Lay: mental and social well-being; WHO: absence of disease"],"e":"Lay conceptions: absence of disease (objective), absence of illness (subjective), functional fitness. WHO: complete <b>physical, mental and social</b> well-being, <b>not merely</b> absence of disease or infirmity.","w":["Lay includes absence of illness and functional fitness.","Lay includes absence of disease and illness.","They differ.","Reversed."],"k":"Compare lay and WHO definitions.","rn":[["","What are the 3 lay conceptions of health?","What are the 3 lay conceptions of health?","Absence of disease; Absence of illness; Functional fitness"]]},{"id":5098,"t":"The Biopsychosocial Model","s":"Which factor belongs in the biological domain of the biopsychosocial model?","o":["Stress reactivity","Self-esteem","Peer relationships","Learning and memory","Family background"],"e":"BIO circle: gender, disability, physical health, neurochemistry, <b>stress reactivity</b>, genetic vulnerability (and sex, in the recording).","w":["Psych.","Social.","Psych.","Social."],"k":"Stress reactivity = biological.","rn":[["","Biological circle examples?","Biological circle examples?","Gender; Disability; Physical health; Neurochemistry; Stress reactivity; Genetic vulnerability"]]},{"id":5099,"t":"Biology and Society","s":"Which event was hailed as “The Century of Genetics” (Duster, 2015)?","o":["The 2000 genome draft was hailed “The Century of Genetics” (Duster, 2015)","The 1865 laws of heredity were hailed “The Century of Genetics” (Duster, 2015)","The 1942 Modern Synthesis was hailed “The Century of Genetics” (Duster, 2015)","The 2000 genome draft was hailed “The Century of Genetics” (Engel)","The 2000 genome draft was hailed “The Century of Genetics” (Nettleton, 2021)"],"e":"<b>2000</b>: completion of the <b>first draft of the Human Genome Map</b>, hailed “<b>The Century of Genetics</b>” (Duster, 2015).","w":["2000.","2000.","Duster.","Duster."],"k":"2000 genome draft = “Century of Genetics”.","rn":[["","What happened in 2000?","What happened in 2000?","First draft of the Human Genome Map (\"The Century of Genetics\")"]]},{"id":5100,"t":"Social Factors in Disease","s":"How have police responses been relevant to mental health?","o":["Police have reacted badly, missing a mental health condition","Police have reacted well, recognising learning disabilities and conditions quickly","Police make most mental health diagnoses in emergencies","Police reactions vary only by socioeconomic status","Police are responsible for mental health treatment"],"e":"Treatments for mental health vary by SES, gender and ethnicity. The recording gave cases where <b>police react very badly</b> to someone with a mental health condition, assuming they are dangerous, or not recognising a mental health condition or learning disability.","w":["The opposite was described.","Not stated.","Not the point.","Not stated."],"k":"Social responses shape mental health outcomes.","rn":[["","How can social assumptions affect a person with a mental health condition?","How can social assumptions affect a person with a mental health condition?","Police may assume they are dangerous; Condition or learning disability not recognised"]]},{"id":5101,"t":"Epigenetics","s":"A student writes “biology determines race, and race determines disease”. Which study best challenges this?","o":["Kuzawa and Sweet (2009)","Tegethoff et al. (2010)","Jasienska (2009)","Maguire et al. (2006)","Beecher (1955)"],"e":"Kuzawa and Sweet argue <b>biology is the outcome of racialisation rather than the underpinning of race</b> (see Ossorio and Duster 2005; Krieger 2005).","w":["Placental weight.","Slavery and LBW (related, but the race/biology argument is Kuzawa and Sweet).","Taxi drivers.","Placebo."],"k":"Racialisation → biology (Kuzawa & Sweet).","rn":[["","What did Kuzawa and Sweet (2009) say about race and biology?","What did Kuzawa and Sweet (2009) say about race and biology?","Biology is the outcome of racialisation; Not the underpinning of race"],["","What did Kuzawa and Sweet (2009) conclude?","What did Kuzawa and Sweet (2009) conclude?","Race as a social construct causes real harm; Biology is the outcome of racialisation, not the basis of race"]]},{"id":5102,"t":"Pain, Placebo and Nocebo","s":"Which factor in the pain model is <b>biological</b>?","o":["Nociception","Catastrophizing","Economic factors","Coping","Social support"],"e":"Biological factors in pain: <b>disease severity, nociception, inflammation, brain function</b>.","w":["Psychological.","Social.","Psychological.","Social."],"k":"Nociception = biological.","rn":[["","What are the biological factors in pain?","What are the biological factors in pain?","Disease severity; Nociception; Inflammation; Brain function"]]},{"id":5103,"t":"The Biopsychosocial Model","s":"Which item is an example of the “psychological + social” overlap in the biopsychosocial model?","o":["Life events and their timing","Substance abuse","Genetic vulnerability","Coping skills","Neurochemistry"],"e":"Social + psych overlap: <b>family relationships and life events</b>, including the particular timing of life events.","w":["Bio + social.","Biological.","Psychological.","Biological."],"k":"Social + psych: family relationships, life events.","rn":[["","Family relationships and life events sit in which overlap?","Family relationships and life events sit in which overlap?","Social + psychological"],["","Why can family relationships and life events be both social and psychological?","Why can family relationships and life events be both social and psychological?","They sit in the social + psychological overlap"]]},{"id":5104,"t":"Pain, Placebo and Nocebo","s":"What term describes a measurable improvement in health that cannot be attributed to the treatment given?","e":"The placebo effect: measurable improvement not attributable to the treatment; Beecher (1955) 32%, later 50–60%; greater when people choose their treatment.","k":"Placebo effect.","a":["placebo","placebo effect","placebo response"],"rn":[["","Placebo effect","Placebo effect","Measurable improvement in health; Not attributable to the treatment"]]},{"id":5105,"t":"Value and Limits of the Biopsychosocial Model","s":"Which is the best description of “reflexivity” in practice?","o":["A practitioner reflecting on their own practice","A patient reflecting on their own adherence to treatment","A practitioner adapting treatment to the latest guidelines","A practitioner referring the patient to another specialist","A patient reflecting on how their illness affects their family life"],"e":"The model enables <b>practitioner reflexivity</b>: practitioners can <b>reflect</b> on their treatment of a patient and tailor advice to the individual.","w":["Practitioner.","Not the definition.","Not the definition.","Practitioner, not patient."],"k":"Reflexivity = reflecting on own practice.","rn":[["","Reflexivity","Reflexivity","Practitioner reflecting on their own practice with a patient"]]},{"id":5106,"t":"The Biopsychosocial Model","s":"Which statement about “living and working conditions” and stress is correct?","o":["Education and work environment influence stress","Education and work environment affect only infectious disease","Education and work environment are biological factors","Education and work environment are the innermost layer","Education and work environment have little effect on health"],"e":"The lecturer noted that <b>education and work environment all influence stress</b>, part of the living and working conditions layer.","w":["Stress too.","Social factors.","Individual factors are innermost.","They do."],"k":"Work and education influence stress.","rn":[["","What are examples of living and working conditions?","What are examples of living and working conditions?","Agriculture and food; Education; Work environment, unemployment; Water and sanitation; Health care services; Housing"],["","Why is the fall in deaths not just biochemical?","Why is the fall in deaths not just biochemical?","Technology explains only some; Work, stress, environment, poverty, diet matter"]]},{"id":5107,"t":"The Biopsychosocial Model","s":"Which statement is <b>false</b>?","o":["The biopsychosocial model was proposed by Nettleton","The biomedical model treats the body as a machine","Engel proposed the biopsychosocial model","Nettleton listed five biomedical assumptions","The biomedical model is reductionist"],"e":"<b>Engel</b> proposed the biopsychosocial model; <b>Nettleton (2021)</b> listed the five biomedical assumptions.","w":["True.","True.","True.","True."],"k":"Engel vs Nettleton.","rn":[["","Who proposed the biopsychosocial model?","Who proposed the biopsychosocial model?","George Engel"]]},{"id":5108,"t":"Pain, Placebo and Nocebo","s":"Which of the following would the biomedical model struggle most to explain?","o":["Why a calm environment reduces pain in childbirth","Why antibiotics treat a bacterial infection","Why a broken bone is repaired by surgery","Why infectious deaths fell with vaccination","Why a specific virus causes one specific disease"],"e":"The biomedical model is reductionist, so it struggles with <b>environmental and psychological</b> effects such as reduced childbirth pain in a calm, supportive environment. The others fit its problem-solution logic.","w":["Biomedical.","Biomedical.","Biomedical.","Biomedical (specific aetiology)."],"k":"Social/psychological effects challenge the biomedical model.","rn":[["","What reduces pain in childbirth?","What reduces pain in childbirth?","A calm, supportive environment"]]},{"id":5109,"t":"The Biopsychosocial Model","s":"A patient with chronic back pain from a disc problem also has low mood, catastrophises about the pain, and lost his job last year. A plan that only increases his analgesia most closely reflects which approach, and what does it miss?","o":["Biomedical; it misses psychological and social factors","Biopsychosocial; it misses biological factors","Biomedical; it misses the biological cause","Biopsychosocial; it misses nothing","Biomedical; it misses only the placebo effect"],"e":"Pain has biological (disease severity, nociception, inflammation, brain function), psychological (mood, catastrophising, stress, coping) and social (culture, environment, economic factors, social support) components. Treating only nociception reflects the biomedical model.","w":["Analgesia alone is biomedical, not biopsychosocial.","The analgesia addresses the biological component.","Analgesia alone ignores mood, catastrophising and job loss.","Placebo is only one psychological aspect; the social domain is also missed."],"k":"Pain = bio + psych + social.","rn":[["","What did George Engel's model add to the biomedical model?","What did George Engel's model add to the biomedical model?","Social factors; Psychological factors"]]},{"id":5110,"t":"Biology and Society","s":"Instead of “nature versus nurture”, what phrase describes genes and environment as an interplay?","e":"There is no “versus”: genes and environment always interact – nature by nurture.","k":"Nature by nurture.","a":["nature by nurture","nature and nurture","nature x nurture","gene environment interaction","gene-environment interaction"],"rn":[["","Why is \"nature versus nurture\" meaningless?","Why is \"nature versus nurture\" meaningless?","There is no versus; Genes and environment always interact: nature BY nurture"],["","Why should you never say \"nature versus nurture\"?","Why should you never say \"nature versus nurture\"?","There is no versus; It is an intersection: nature by nurture"]]},{"id":5111,"t":"Pain, Placebo and Nocebo","s":"In a drug trial, 40% of patients given an inert tablet report improvement, and some given the same tablet report side effects they read about in the leaflet. Which pair of terms applies?","o":["Placebo effect; nocebo effect","Nocebo effect; placebo effect","Placebo effect; specific aetiology","Reflexivity; nocebo effect","Placebo effect; mind/body dualism"],"e":"Placebo: a measurable improvement not attributable to the treatment (32% in Beecher 1955; 50–60% since). Nocebo: an inert substance produces symptoms congruent with anticipated harm. Trials must control for placebo effects.","w":["Reversed: improvement is placebo; expected harm is nocebo.","Specific aetiology is about each disease having one cause.","Reflexivity is a practitioner reflecting on their practice.","Dualism separates mind and body – the opposite of what these effects show."],"k":"Placebo helps; nocebo harms through belief.","rn":[["","Nocebo effect","Nocebo effect","Inert substance produces symptoms congruent with anticipated harm"],["","Placebo effect","Placebo effect","Measurable improvement in health; Not attributable to the treatment"],["","How do you remember placebo vs nocebo?","How do you remember placebo vs nocebo?","Placebo = \"I will please\"; Nocebo = \"I will harm\""]]},{"id":5112,"t":"Epigenetics","s":"Beyond work, stress, environment and poverty, which further factor influences cardiovascular health?","o":["The types of diets people are eating","The levels of vaccine uptake","The availability of antibiotics","Genetic susceptibility","Access to screening"],"e":"Cardiovascular health is influenced by work, stress, environment, poverty and <b>the types of diets people are eating</b>.","w":["Infectious disease.","Infectious disease.","Not added.","Not added."],"k":"Diet affects CVD."},{"id":5113,"t":"The Biopsychosocial Model","s":"Which statement best describes how the biopsychosocial model treats the patient?","o":["As an individual in their social, cultural and economic context","As an individual whose disease has one specific, identifiable cause","As a body with a fault to be found and repaired","As a biological system separate from the mind","As a set of genes interacting with infection"],"e":"It <b>sets the patient in their wider social, cultural and economic context</b> – holistic – and treats the patient as an <b>individual, not a machine</b>.","w":["That is biomedical.","Biomedical.","Biomedical.","Not stated."],"k":"BPS: individual in context.","rn":[["","What are the layers of the \"setting biology in context\" model, centre outwards?","What are the layers of the \"setting biology in context\" model, centre outwards?","Age, sex and constitutional factors; Individual lifestyle factors; Social and community networks; Living and working conditions; General socio-economic, cultural and environmental conditions"]]},{"id":5114,"t":"Health, Illness, Disease and Sickness","s":"Someone with arthritis says they are healthy because they can still do their shopping. Name this lay conception of health.","e":"Lay conceptions: absence of disease (objective), absence of illness (subjective), functional fitness (the ability to go on).","k":"Functional fitness.","a":["functional fitness","fitness"],"rn":[["","What are the 3 lay conceptions of health?","What are the 3 lay conceptions of health?","Absence of disease; Absence of illness; Functional fitness"],["","Functional fitness","Functional fitness","Lay idea of health; The ability to go on"]]},{"id":5115,"t":"The Biomedical Model","s":"Which statement about the biomedical model's three possible outcomes is correct?","o":["Recovery, chronic condition, or death","Recovery, relapse, or death","Diagnosis, treatment or discharge","Prevention, screening or treatment","Recovery, chronic condition, or disability"],"e":"After the disease state and treatment, the model shows <b>recovery</b>, a <b>chronic condition that needs management</b>, or <b>death</b>.","w":["Not the three.","Not outcomes.","Interventions.","Death is the third."],"k":"Three biomedical outcomes.","rn":[["","What are the 3 outcomes in the biomedical model?","What are the 3 outcomes in the biomedical model?","Recovery; Chronic condition; Death"]]},{"id":5116,"t":"The Biopsychosocial Model","s":"What is the best reasoning about the rise in circulatory disease from the 1950s?","o":["You must look beyond biology, which is why Engel’s model has value","It was caused by antibiotics changing gut bacteria","It supports the doctrine of specific aetiology","It reflects better diagnosis of heart disease rather than a real change","It shows total deaths doubled after 1950"],"e":"“Why would this increase in the 1950s? <b>You have to start looking more widely</b> for an answer.” This is why <b>Engel's biopsychosocial model</b> provides value.","w":["Not stated.","Challenges it.","Not stated.","Totals did not change dramatically."],"k":"Wider factors explain changing disease.","rn":[["","Who proposed the biopsychosocial model?","Who proposed the biopsychosocial model?","George Engel"],["","What did George Engel's model add to the biomedical model?","What did George Engel's model add to the biomedical model?","Social factors; Psychological factors"]]},{"id":5117,"t":"Epigenetics","s":"Which pair of studies used a <b>historical event</b> and a <b>national birth cohort</b>, respectively?","o":["Jasienska (slavery); Tegethoff et al. (birth cohort 1996–2002)","Tegethoff et al. (slavery); Jasienska (birth cohort 1996–2002)","Kuzawa and Sweet (slavery); Tegethoff et al. (birth cohort)","Jasienska (slavery); Maguire et al. (birth cohort)","Jasienska (slavery); Kuzawa and Sweet (birth cohort)"],"e":"Jasienska looked to a <b>specific historical event, slavery</b>. Tegethoff et al. used a <b>national birth cohort</b> (1996–2002, about 78,000 births).","w":["Reversed.","Jasienska used slavery.","Maguire studied taxi drivers.","Tegethoff used the cohort."],"k":"Jasienska: slavery; Tegethoff: national cohort.","rn":[["","What cohort did Tegethoff et al. (2010) study?","What cohort did Tegethoff et al. (2010) study?","Dutch national birth cohort (1996-2002); About 78,000 births"]]},{"id":5118,"t":"Value and Limits of the Biopsychosocial Model","s":"A doctor tells a patient with poorly controlled diabetes that “your results are bad because you don’t try hard enough”, ignoring the patient’s shift work and food poverty. Which limitation of the biopsychosocial approach does this show?","o":["It can place responsibility for health on individuals","It can be regarded as scientifically feeble “soft science”","It can over-diagnose conditions as psychosomatic","Its research is mainly qualitative","Its coverage in the curriculum varies"],"e":"A limitation is that the model can place responsibility for health on individuals – “you’re not getting well because of factors related to you, not the disease” – rather than wider factors such as work and poverty.","w":["Soft science is a criticism of its evidence base.","No condition is wrongly called psychosomatic here.","That is about methodology.","That is about teaching."],"k":"Risk: blaming the individual.","rn":[["","Why might the biopsychosocial model place blame on individuals?","Why might the biopsychosocial model place blame on individuals?","Suggests ill health relates to the person; Not just to the disease"],["","What are the 5 limitations of the biopsychosocial model?","What are the 5 limitations of the biopsychosocial model?","Puts responsibility for health on individuals; Seen as \"soft science\"; Qualitative methods seen as inferior; Uneven coverage in the curriculum; Over-diagnosis as psychosomatic"]]},{"id":5119,"t":"Value and Limits of the Biopsychosocial Model","s":"A patient with real joint inflammation is told her pain is “all in her head” because she has anxiety. Which limitation of the biopsychosocial model does this illustrate?","o":["Over-diagnosing conditions as psychosomatic","Placing responsibility for health on individuals","Being regarded as scientifically feeble","Mind/body dualism","Prioritising technological responses"],"e":"Because the model links biology and psychology, a condition really occurring in the body may be wrongly diagnosed as psychosomatic.","w":["That is about blaming the patient’s behaviour.","That is about the evidence base.","Dualism is a biomedical assumption.","That is a biomedical assumption."],"k":"Risk: wrongly labelling bodily disease psychosomatic.","rn":[["","What does \"over-diagnosing as psychosomatic\" mean?","What does \"over-diagnosing as psychosomatic\" mean?","Calling a condition mental, not in the body; Because biology and psychology are linked"]]},{"id":5120,"t":"Neuroplasticity","s":"Which combination best summarises the “new biologies”?","o":["Epigenetics: environment affects gene expression; neuroplasticity: the brain rewires","Epigenetics: environment changes DNA sequence; neuroplasticity: the brain is fixed","Epigenetics: genes determine behaviour; neuroplasticity: the brain rewires","Epigenetics: environment affects gene expression; neuroplasticity: only children rewire","Epigenetics: phrenology revisited; neuroplasticity: eugenics revisited"],"e":"<b>Epigenetics</b>: the environment shapes gene expression, with studies on stress across generations (Tegethoff, Kuzawa and Sweet, Jasienska). <b>Neuroplasticity</b>: the brain can rewire (infant stroke case, taxi drivers). Both show biology becoming more social.","w":["Not sequence; brain is plastic.","Not deterministic.","Adults too.","Not the case."],"k":"New biologies: epigenetics + neuroplasticity.","rn":[["","Neuroplasticity","Neuroplasticity","The brain can be re-organised or rewired"],["","What are the 2 \"new biologies\"?","What are the 2 \"new biologies\"?","Epigenetics; Neuroplasticity"],["","What do epigenetic modifications change?","What do epigenetic modifications change?","Gene expression"]]},{"id":5121,"t":"Health, Illness, Disease and Sickness","s":"A 72-year-old man with well-controlled arthritis and high blood pressure attends a routine review. He says he considers himself \"perfectly healthy\" because he can still walk his dog, do his gardening and look after his grandchildren. His blood tests show his diseases are still present. His GP notes that his view of health differs from a purely medical one.<br><br>Which lay conception of health is he using, and why does it differ from the medical view?","o":["Functional fitness: he can still go on, even though disease is present","Absence of disease: he has no objective signs, so he feels healthy","Absence of illness: he has no diseases, so he is healthy by definition","The WHO definition: he has complete physical, mental and social well-being, despite his diseases","The biomedical model: his body can be repaired like a machine"],"e":"Step 1: lay conceptions of health include absence of disease (objective symptoms), absence of illness (subjective symptoms) and functional fitness (the ability to go on). Step 2: he calls himself healthy because he can still go on and keep a good quality of life, which is functional fitness; this differs from the medical view because his diseases are objectively still present.","w":["His diseases are objectively present, so it cannot be absence of disease.","Absence of illness refers to subjective symptoms, and he does have diseases.","The WHO definition is an official definition, not a lay conception.","The biomedical model is a medical approach, not a lay conception of health."],"k":"Functional fitness = health as the ability to go on.","rn":[["","What are the 3 lay conceptions of health?","What are the 3 lay conceptions of health?","Absence of disease; Absence of illness; Functional fitness"],["","Why can the lay idea \"functional fitness\" count as health?","Why can the lay idea \"functional fitness\" count as health?","Still able to go on; Still having a good quality of life"],["","Functional fitness","Functional fitness","Lay idea of health; The ability to go on"]]},{"id":5122,"t":"The Biomedical Model","s":"A 50-year-old woman with chronic back pain sees a specialist. He orders a scan, finds a small disc problem and recommends surgery. He tells her that her recent bereavement and job loss are irrelevant to her pain, which has \"one physical cause\" that can be fixed with the right technology. She is unhappy and feels unheard.<br><br>Which assumptions of the biomedical model is the specialist showing?","o":["Mind/body dualism, a technological response and specific aetiology","Holism, empathetic practice and patient-centred care","Mind–body monism, practitioner reflexivity and tailoring of advice to the person","Nature by nurture and neuroplasticity","Functional fitness and absence of illness"],"e":"Step 1: Nettleton's five assumptions of the biomedical model are mind/body dualism, the mechanical metaphor (the body can be repaired), technological responses, reductionism and the doctrine of specific aetiology. Step 2: dismissing her bereavement and job loss is mind/body dualism, preferring surgery after a scan is a technological response, and insisting on \"one physical cause\" is specific aetiology.","w":["These are strengths of the biopsychosocial model, which he is not using.","Monism and reflexivity belong to the biopsychosocial approach.","These are ideas from the new biologies section, not biomedical assumptions.","These are lay conceptions of health."],"k":"Biomedical: dualism, mechanical, technological, reductionist, specific aetiology.","rn":[["","What are Nettleton's (2021) 5 assumptions of the biomedical model?","What are Nettleton's (2021) 5 assumptions of the biomedical model?","Mind/body dualism; Mechanical metaphor (body can be repaired); Prioritises technological responses; Reductionist (biological focus); Doctrine of specific aetiology; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":5123,"t":"Pain, Placebo and Nocebo","s":"A public health student compares causes of death in England and Wales in 1900 and 1997. Infectious diseases caused a much larger share of deaths in 1900, while circulatory diseases and cancers made up a much larger share by 1997. The student concludes that better biochemistry and drugs alone explain this change.<br><br>Which response best reflects the lecture's view?","o":["The change is not simply biochemical; work, stress, environment and poverty also matter","The change proves the biomedical model is complete and needs no other perspectives","The change is caused by germ theory, so specific aetiology explains it fully","The change shows that infectious diseases are now caused mainly by stress, poverty and the conditions of work","The change shows that life expectancy and healthy life expectancy are the same"],"e":"Step 1: the causes-of-death figure shows infections falling and circulatory diseases and cancers rising between 1900 and 1997. Step 2: the lecture states this changing disease profile is not simply the result of biochemical factors; it is influenced by work, stress, environment and poverty, so behaviour and wider societal factors must be considered.","w":["The lecture uses this change to show what the biomedical model misses.","Specific aetiology alone cannot account for the change.","The lecture does not say infections are now mainly caused by stress.","The lecture distinguishes life expectancy from healthy life expectancy."],"k":"Disease profile change is not just biochemical: social factors matter.","rn":[["","Why is the fall in deaths not just biochemical?","Why is the fall in deaths not just biochemical?","Technology explains only some; Work, stress, environment, poverty, diet matter"],["","What else explains the changing disease profile?","What else explains the changing disease profile?","Work, stress, environment, poverty, diet; Not just biochemical factors"]]},{"id":5124,"t":"The Biopsychosocial Model","s":"A 19-year-old student has a family history suggesting a genetic susceptibility to alcohol problems and tends to take risks. He grew up in a remote area where alcohol was hard to get and never drank heavily. After moving to a city with cheap bars, he begins drinking heavily. His tutor uses the biopsychosocial model to explain this.<br><br>Where does substance abuse sit in the model, and why?","o":["Bio + social overlap: a predisposition only leads to abuse if there is access","Psych + social overlap: family relationships and the timing of life events","Bio + psych overlap: IQ and temperament","Biological circle only: genetic vulnerability alone causes abuse, whatever the social environment is like","Social circle only: peer relationships alone explain it"],"e":"Step 1: the worked example of the biopsychosocial model places substance abuse in the overlap of the biological and social circles. Step 2: there may be a biological predisposition (risk-taking, genetic susceptibility to alcohol), but if a person never has access to the substance there will be no abuse, so the social environment matters too, as his move to the city shows.","w":["Family relationships and life events are the psych + social overlap.","IQ and temperament are the bio + psych overlap.","He did not drink heavily when alcohol was hard to get, so biology alone is not enough.","His genetic susceptibility also matters, so it is not social alone."],"k":"Substance abuse = bio + social: predisposition needs access.","rn":[["","Why is substance abuse bio + social?","Why is substance abuse bio + social?","Needs a biological predisposition; And access to the substance (social environment)"]]},{"id":5125,"t":"Social Factors in Disease","s":"A 58-year-old woman attends the emergency department with chest pain. She is told her symptoms are probably stress-related and sent home, but a week later she is diagnosed with coronary heart disease. Her 60-year-old husband has had chest pain for months but has not seen a doctor.<br><br>Which statement best explains both situations, using the lecture?","o":["Women are less likely to be diagnosed with CHD, and men are less likely to consult generally","Diagnosis is purely biological, so both delays were due to chance","Men are more likely to be diagnosed with anxiety, so they avoid doctors","The nocebo effect caused her pain, because she expected to be harmed by attending hospital","Trust in doctors reduces consultation in men"],"e":"Step 1: the lecture states that diagnosis is socially and historically located, not purely biological. Step 2: women are less likely to be diagnosed with coronary heart disease (CHD) than men, which fits her missed diagnosis, while men are less likely to consult generally, which fits her husband's delay.","w":["The lecture says diagnosis is socially located, not purely biological.","The lecture does not say men are more often diagnosed with anxiety.","The nocebo effect is symptoms from an inert substance expected to cause harm.","The lecture says trust enhances therapy and adherence."],"k":"Women less often diagnosed with CHD; men consult less.","rn":[["","Who is less likely to be diagnosed with coronary heart disease?","Who is less likely to be diagnosed with coronary heart disease?","Women (though men consult less)"]]},{"id":5126,"t":"Pain, Placebo and Nocebo","s":"In a trial, patients with mild depression are given either an active antidepressant or an identical inert tablet. Many in both groups improve, and improvement is greater in those who were allowed to choose their treatment. Separately, some patients told that a harmless tablet may cause headaches report headaches after taking it.<br><br>Which pair of effects is shown?","o":["Placebo effect (greater with choice), then nocebo effect","Nocebo effect in the trial, then placebo effect from the tablet","Specific aetiology, then the mechanical metaphor","Placebo effect in both, because inert tablets cannot produce harm in any patient","Neuroplasticity in the trial, then epigenetics"],"e":"Step 1: improvement on an inert tablet is the placebo effect; the lecture notes that Prozac for mild depression is largely a placebo effect, and the effect is greater if people choose their \"treatment\". Step 2: the nocebo effect occurs when an inert substance produces symptoms congruent with anticipated harm, which matches the headaches.","w":["The labels are reversed.","These are biomedical assumptions, not psychological effects.","An inert substance can produce harm through expectation: the nocebo effect.","These are new biologies, not effects of belief about a treatment."],"k":"Placebo = benefit from inert treatment; nocebo = harm from expected harm.","rn":[["","When is the placebo effect greater?","When is the placebo effect greater?","When people choose the treatment (Rose et al., 2012)"],["","Nocebo effect","Nocebo effect","Inert substance produces symptoms congruent with anticipated harm"]]},{"id":5127,"t":"Pain, Placebo and Nocebo","s":"A 40-year-old woman has chronic knee pain from inflammation. Her pain is much worse during a stressful period when she keeps thinking the pain means something terrible will happen. It is also worse because she cannot afford to take time off work and has little support at home. Her doctor explains her pain using a model with three groups of factors.<br><br>Which grouping of her factors is correct?","o":["Inflammation: biological; catastrophising and stress: psychological; economic factors and support: social","Inflammation: psychological; stress: biological; support: social","Catastrophising: social; inflammation: biological; economic factors: psychological","All three are biological, because pain comes from nociception and inflammation only","Stress: social; support: psychological; inflammation: social"],"e":"Step 1: the lecture applies the biopsychosocial model to pain, with biological (disease severity, nociception, inflammation, brain function), psychological (mood, catastrophising, stress, coping) and social (culture, social environment, economic factors, social support) factors. Step 2: her inflammation is biological, catastrophising and stress are psychological, and money worries and poor support are social.","w":["Inflammation is biological and stress is psychological.","Catastrophising is psychological and economic factors are social.","The model includes psychological and social factors in pain.","Stress is psychological and support is social."],"k":"Pain: bio (inflammation), psych (catastrophising, stress), social (economics, support).","rn":[["","What are the social factors in pain?","What are the social factors in pain?","Cultural factors; Social environment; Economic factors; Social support"],["","What 7 types of factor shape health status?","What 7 types of factor shape health status?","Social; Cultural; Historical; Economic; Psychological; Biological; Environmental; MNEMONIC"],["","What are the biological factors in pain?","What are the biological factors in pain?","Disease severity; Nociception; Inflammation; Brain function"]]},{"id":5128,"t":"Value and Limits of the Biopsychosocial Model","s":"A junior doctor uses the biopsychosocial model with every patient. A colleague criticises her: she tells an obese patient that his weight is entirely his own fault, and she often labels unexplained symptoms as psychosomatic. Another colleague says the model is \"soft science\" because its research is mostly qualitative.<br><br>Which limitations of the model are being described?","o":["Responsibility placed on individuals, psychosomatic over-diagnosis and being seen as scientifically feeble","Mind/body dualism, reductionism and specific aetiology","Holism, empathy and practitioner reflexivity","Patient-centred care and tailored advice to each patient's social and economic context","Doctrine of specific aetiology and the mechanical metaphor"],"e":"Step 1: the limitations of the biopsychosocial model include placing responsibility for health on individuals, being regarded as scientifically feeble (\"soft science\"), qualitative methods seen as inferior, uneven curriculum coverage and over-diagnosing conditions as psychosomatic. Step 2: blaming the patient, labelling symptoms psychosomatic and calling it soft science match three of these.","w":["These are assumptions of the biomedical model.","These are strengths of the biopsychosocial model.","These are strengths, not limitations.","These are biomedical assumptions."],"k":"BPS limits: individual blame, soft science, qualitative, psychosomatic over-diagnosis.","rn":[["","What are the 5 limitations of the biopsychosocial model?","What are the 5 limitations of the biopsychosocial model?","Puts responsibility for health on individuals; Seen as \"soft science\"; Qualitative methods seen as inferior; Uneven coverage in the curriculum; Over-diagnosis as psychosomatic"]]},{"id":5129,"t":"Epigenetics","s":"A researcher studies low birth weight (below 2.5 kg) in a population. African American women with slave ancestry have smaller babies than black women born in African countries ancestral to slave populations who now live in the United States. She proposes that undernutrition and hard labour in the slavery period still affect birth weight today.<br><br>Which concept and hypothesis does this illustrate?","o":["Epigenetics: the slavery hypothesis (Jasienska)","Neuroplasticity: the taxi-driver study (Maguire)","Germ theory: the doctrine of specific aetiology","Nature versus nurture: genes alone explain birth weight differences across all populations","The nocebo effect: expected harm causing low birth weight"],"e":"Step 1: epigenetics describes how the environment shapes how much, or whether, genes are expressed, and how changes may be passed on. Step 2: Jasienska's slavery hypothesis proposes that life conditions in the slavery period (maternal undernutrition and intense labour) are responsible for the low birth weight of African Americans today, supported by the comparison with women of African birth.","w":["The taxi-driver study is about hippocampal enlargement.","Specific aetiology is a biomedical assumption from germ theory.","The lecture rejects \"nature versus nurture\" in favour of nature by nurture.","Nocebo is harm from belief about an inert substance."],"k":"Slavery hypothesis = epigenetics (history → birth weight).","rn":[["","What did Jasienska (2009) propose?","What did Jasienska (2009) propose?","The slavery hypothesis; Slavery-era conditions cause low birth weight in African Americans today"]]},{"id":5130,"t":"Neuroplasticity","s":"A 45-year-old has worked as a London taxi driver for 20 years and has learned thousands of routes by heart. In a research study, MRI scans of drivers like him show that part of the brain involved in memory is substantially larger than expected. A student argues that adult brains are fixed and cannot change.<br><br>Which concept answers the student, and which brain region was enlarged?","o":["Neuroplasticity; the hippocampi","Epigenetics; the cerebellum","Neuroplasticity; the brainstem","Mind/body dualism; the frontal lobes","The placebo effect; the hippocampi, because the drivers expected their brains to grow larger"],"e":"Step 1: neuroplasticity means the brain is \"plastic\": it can be reorganised or rewired, with new connections forming and old ones rewired. Step 2: Maguire et al. (2006) used magnetic resonance on London cab drivers and found substantial enlargement of the volume of their hippocampi, showing extensive driving can cause structural brain change.","w":["Epigenetics concerns gene expression, and the cerebellum was not the region studied.","The brainstem was not the region studied.","Dualism is a biomedical assumption, not a brain-change concept.","The change was structural, not a placebo effect."],"k":"Taxi drivers: neuroplasticity → enlarged hippocampi.","rn":[["","What did Maguire et al. (2006) find in London taxi drivers?","What did Maguire et al. (2006) find in London taxi drivers?","Substantially enlarged hippocampi"],["","What did Maguire et al. (2006) study?","What did Maguire et al. (2006) study?","London cab drivers; MRI of hippocampi"]]}]);
